as a fallback for web browsing.\\n   - When WebSearch tool fails or returns insufficient results, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for internet search.\\n   - When you need to interact with dynamic web pages that require JavaScript execution, use Playwright MCP tools.\\n   - When you need to visually verify how a web page looks or take screenshots, use browser_take_screenshot from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you need to fill forms, click buttons, or perform user interactions on web pages, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_click, browser_type, browser_fill_form).\\n   - When you need to test responsive design or different viewport sizes, use browser_resize from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you finish using the browser, close it with browser_close to free resources.\\n   - When reproducing UI bugs, use browser_take_screenshot to capture the problem state before implementing any fix.\\n   - When fixing UI bugs, take before/after screenshots to provide visual evidence of the fix for human verification.\\n   - When creating UI tests, save baseline screenshots to the repository for visual regression testing.\\n   - When verifying UI fixes, compare screenshots to ensure the fix does not introduce unintended visual changes.\\nIssue completion goal.\\n   - Do not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\n   - Try to set this exact objective with a native goal-setting API or tool before working, and keep it active until every acceptance criterion is verified. Codex exposes thread/goal/set through its app-server API when available; do not invent an exec --goal flag. If the running tool provides no native goal API, keep the objective and checklist in your work plan across turns and compaction.\\n   - Read the complete issue, every comment, every required sub-issue, and all pull request conversation comments, inline comments, and reviews. Inventory each requirement, including prose requirements and later feedback, and map each one to implementation and verification evidence.\\n   - Passing CI, a partial implementation, an upstream report, or a workaround does not complete an unmet acceptance criterion. Continue authorized work. If an external blocker cannot be resolved, report the exact blocked criteria and evidence, leave them unfinished, and keep the pull request unmerged.\\n   - Preserve a positive closing keyword in the pull request description for the original issue and every required issue. Repeat the keyword per issue, use the correct repository for foreign issues, and keep references outside examples or code blocks. A negated sentence (for example \\\"does not close\\\" before an issue reference) is not a closing reference. Mention issues you reported to other repositories without a keyword: GitHub also closes an issue linked right after \\\"fix:\\\", as in \\\"a suggested fix: <issue URL>\\\".\\nLocation privacy: Keep the user's and host's geolocation private. Avoid collecting, inferring, or transmitting their location, including IP-geolocation lookups, GPS/browser location permissions, local timezone or region probes, and user_location hints in search or model requests. Use location-neutral searches. Propagate this policy to delegated agents and browser/MCP tools. Locations explicitly present in task content may be used to discuss that content; they do not authorize discovery or disclosure of the user's or host's location.\\nIssue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1584\\nYour prepared branch: issue-1584-49c73d39af58\\nYour prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794915285086\\nYour prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/1585\\nMerge status is UNSTABLE (non-passing commit status)\\nFailed pull request checks: 1\\nCollect data related about the issue to this repository, make sure we compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/1584/pulls/1585 folder.\\nDo not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\nContinue.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me run the requested command to get that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_fec4fd7162da5053\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1584' --json title --jq .title && echo && gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1584' --json body --jq .body; printf '\\\\n__formal_ai_exit=%s\\\\n' $?\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_fec4fd7162da5053\",\"content\":\"feat: Unrestricted input with configurable CNL output standards, lexical density spectrum, and cross-standard testing\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nIntroduce a configurable **Controlled Natural Language (CNL) output and clarification system** across all supported languages in Formal AI (`formalize → summarize → deformalize` pipeline, CLI, HTTP API, desktop, and WebAssembly worker), while preserving **100% unrestricted natural-language input**.\\n\\n1. **Unrestricted Input:** Formal AI must continue to accept any arbitrary user input—free-form prose, slang, typos, stream-of-consciousness notes, or any strict CNL dialect—without rejecting non-conforming phrasing.\\n2. **Configurable Output Standards & Lexical Spectrum:** By default, responses follow the intersection of established CNL standards (such as **ASD-STE100**, **Attempto Controlled English (ACE)**, **Plain Language ISO 24495-1**, **Gellish**, and **Minimal English**, plus localized equivalents) for maximum clarity and simplicity. Users can also explicitly request output in **any specific CNL standard** or anywhere along a **lexical density spectrum**—from **NSM Semantic Primes only** (65 primitives) up to **Maximum Lexical Density** (using rare, ultra-specific single words with a dictionary-lookup notice).\\n3. **Cross-Standard Matrix Testing:** Expand the test suite so identical intents are formulated across all supported CNL standards and free-form styles as inputs, verifying deterministic behavior across every `input style × output standard` combination.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Motivation\\n\\n1. **Zero friction on input, maximum clarity on output:** Users should never be forced to memorize a rigid grammar just to talk to Formal AI. However, Formal AI's responses should be predictable, easy to read, and free of ambiguity.\\n2. **Alignment with deterministic symbolic reasoning:** Formal AI maps input into inspectable Links Notation graphs rather than probabilistic neural weights. Realizing those internal graphs through strict CNL projection rules ensures one concept maps to one canonical term, using active voice, short sentences, and zero filler.\\n3. **Adaptable lexical granularity & named standards:** Different workflows require different output constraints—from strict logic-mappable dialects (`ACE`, `SBVR`, `Gellish`), aviation-grade clarity (`ASD-STE100`), ultra-simple primitives (`NSM Primes`, `Top-100/200/500/1000`), to hyper-compressed expert vocabulary (`max-precision`).\\n4. **Disambiguating stream-of-consciousness input:** When free-form input maps to multiple valid intent graphs, Formal AI should reformulate the candidate interpretations into precise CNL statements and ask the user to confirm before acting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Proposed Features & Requirements\\n\\n#### 1. Unrestricted Input Handling (Postel's Law)\\n- Continue accepting any free-form text, incomplete thoughts, mixed dialects, or formal CNL queries without imposing input grammar walls.\\n- Normalizers and intent formalizers (`rust/src/intent_formalization.rs`) should parse both unformatted everyday speech and strict CNL dialects into the same underlying Links Notation representation.\\n\\n#### 2. Default Output: Intersection of CNL Standards (Multilingual)\\n- Set the default output style (`cnl-default`) to the common intersection of **ASD-STE100**, **Plain Language (ISO 24495-1)**, **ACE**, and **Minimal English** across all supported languages (English, Russian, Hindi, Chinese, and localized equivalents such as *Leichte Sprache*, *Ясный язык*, etc.):\\n  - Prioritize simple, high-frequency, unambiguous words (one word = one meaning).\\n  - Use short sentences, active voice, and explicit subject-verb-object structure.\\n  - Eliminate ambiguous pronouns, idioms, and redundant filler.\\n\\n#### 3. Support for Specific Named CNL Standards on Output\\nUsers must be able to request responses formatted strictly according to any supported CNL standard (globally via config or per-turn in chat):\\n- `asd-ste100` — Simplified Technical English (approved vocabulary, active voice, max sentence length).\\n- `ace` — Attempto Controlled English (unambiguous phrasing mappable to first-order logic).\\n- `gellish` — Formalized triple-oriented English/Dutch relation phrasing.\\n- `sbvr` — Structured English for business rules and constraints.\\n- `plain-language` — ISO 24495-1 / Plain English.\\n- `minimal-english` — NSM-based Minimal English (and localized equivalents like *Minimal Polish*, *Minimal French*, *65 Sanaa*).\\n- `e-prime` — English without any forms of the verb *to be*.\\n- `basic-english` / `special-english` / `globish` — Controlled core vocabularies (~850–1500 words).\\n- `cnl-default` (Default) — Balanced intersection of the standards above.\\n\\n#### 4. Configurable Lexical Density Spectrum\\nIn addition to named standards, support a continuous/stepped vocabulary compression spectrum:\\n- `primes` — **NSM Semantic Primes only** (~65 universal primitives from [Natural Semantic Metalanguage](https://wikipedia.org) + minimal syntax frames).\\n- `top-100` / `top-200` / `top-500` / `top-1000` (and arbitrary `top-N` tiers) — Restrict output to the N most frequent unambiguous words, decomposing complex concepts into simpler primitives/molecules.\\n- `standard` — Unrestricted everyday vocabulary.\\n- `dense` / `max-precision` — **Maximum Lexical Density mode**:\\n  - Prioritize existing, maximally capacious, exact, and specific words (even if rare, archaic, or highly technical) to convey the full meaning in the **minimum possible number of words**.\\n  - Explicitly notify the user when `max-precision` mode is active that hyper-specific vocabulary is used and recommend looking up unfamiliar words in a dictionary for their exact definitions.\\n\\n#### 5. In-Dialog Dynamic Switching & Bidirectional Paraphrasing\\nUsers can switch standards/modes globally (`lino-arguments`, CLI flags, API params, UI) or request transformations mid-dialog:\\n- **Explicit standard requests:** e.g., `\\\"Answer in Attempto Controlled English\\\"`, `\\\"Use ASD-STE100\\\"`, `\\\"Respond in E-Prime\\\"`.\\n- **On-Demand Simplification (Decompression):** e.g., `\\\"Explain this in simpler words\\\"`, `\\\"Rewrite using semantic primes only\\\"`, `\\\"Explain using top 200 words\\\"`.\\n- **On-Demand Compression (Densification):** e.g., `\\\"Say this in the fewest, most exact words possible\\\"`, `\\\"Rewrite in max-precision mode\\\"`.\\n\\n#### 6. CNL-Guided Clarification for Ambiguous Prompts\\n- When unrestricted user input (such as a stream of consciousness) maps to multiple plausible intent graphs in `rust/src/solver_unknown_reasoning.rs`:\\n  - Restate each candidate interpretation using **precise CNL phrasing** (clearer and more exact than the user's original input) and ask the user to confirm or clarify.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Expanded Cross-Standard Testing Matrix\\n\\nExpand the test suites (both Rust unit/integration tests and JavaScript/WASM parity tests) to evaluate **Input × Output** invariance and compliance:\\n- **Multi-Standard Input Corpus:** Formulate the same canonical prompts across every supported standard and style:\\n  - Free-form / colloquial / stream-of-consciousness input\\n  - NSM Semantic Primes\\n  - ASD-STE100\\n  - Attempto Controlled English (ACE)\\n  - Gellish / SBVR Structured English\\n  - Plain Language / Basic English / E-Prime\\n  - High-density / rare-word technical phrasing\\n- **Verification Goals:**\\n  1. **Input Equivalence:** Confirm that phrasing the same intent in any of the standards above resolves to the exact same internal Links Notation intent/meaning graph.\\n  2. **Output Compliance:** Verify that for any input, requesting any target output standard or lexical tier (`primes`, `top-N`, `asd-ste100`, `ace`, `cnl-default`, `max-precision`, etc.) produces valid, deterministic output conforming to that standard's constraints.\\n  3. **Round-Trip Consistency:** Test `free-form → formal Lino → dense → primes → cnl-default` round-trips to ensure zero semantic drift.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] Unrestricted natural-language input continues to work seamlessly alongside all CNL dialects.\\n- [ ] Default responses (`cnl-default`) across supported languages follow the intersection of ASD-STE100 / Plain Language / CNL clarity rules.\\n- [ ] Users can request output in specific named CNL standards (`asd-ste100`, `ace`, `gellish`, `sbvr`, `e-prime`, `minimal-english`, `plain-language`, `basic-english`) or across the lexical density spectrum (`primes`, `top-100..1000`, `cnl-default`, `standard`, `max-precision`).\\n- [ ] `max-precision` mode uses the fewest, most exact rare/specific words possible and notifies the user to consult a dictionary for unfamiliar terms.\\n- [ ] Bidirectional in-dialog paraphrasing (simple ↔ dense, or switching standards mid-chat) works deterministically.\\n- [ ] Ambiguous free-form prompts trigger a CNL-phrased clarification turn restating candidate interpretations more precisely.\\n- [ ] Expanded test matrix covers identical intents formulated across all supported input standards paired with all output standards in both Rust and JS/WASM.\\n\\n\\n__formal_ai_exit=0\\n\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794915285086/rust/src/intent_formalization.rs and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_b2b541e21b420491\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794915285086/rust/src/intent_formalization.rs\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_b2b541e21b420491\",\"content\":\"<file>\\n00001| //! Links-Notation intent formalization and cache.\\n00002| //!\\n00003| //! The P/Q formalization layer identifies meaning anchors inside a prompt.\\n00004| //! This module wraps those anchors in the routing-facing intent record: what\\n00005| //! kind of impulse arrived, what is already known, and which handlers or rules\\n00006| //! are relevant.\\n00007| \\n00008| use std::collections::BTreeMap;\\n00009| use std::sync::OnceLock;\\n00010| \\n00011| use lino_objects_codec::format::escape_reference;\\n00012| \\n00013| use crate::engine::{\\n00014|     SelectedRule, WRITE_PROGRAM_INTENT, normalize_prompt, program_spec, stable_id,\\n00015| };\\n00016| use crate::event_log::EventLog;\\n00017| use crate::link_store::{LinkStore, LinkStoreError};\\n00018| use crate::links_format::format_lino_record;\\n00019| use crate::memory::MemoryEvent;\\n00020| use crate::method_registry::MethodRegistry;\\n00021| use crate::probability::ProbabilityStore;\\n00022| use crate::seed;\\n00023| use crate::solver::{ConversationTurn, UniversalSolver};\\n00024| use crate::translation::{FormalizationAnchorKind, FormalizationCandidate, FormalizationRole};\\n00025| \\n00026| mod obligations;\\n00027| mod prompt_relevants;\\n00028| mod requirements;\\n00029| mod write_program_request;\\n00030| pub use obligations::{\\n00031|     OBLIGATION_GAP_KIND, ObligationGraph, ObligationKind, bound_output_literals, coreference_pass,\\n00032|     formalize_request, obligation_gap_lines, record_obligation_gaps,\\n00033|     request_carries_work_obligations, request_demands,\\n00034| };\\n00035| pub(crate) use obligations::{describes_a_value, explicit_count, unquoted_utterance};\\n00036| use prompt_relevants::append_prompt_relevants;\\n00037| pub use requirements::{OrderedRequirementSpan, ordered_requirement_spans};\\n00038| pub(crate) use requirements::{requirement_list_spans, requirement_operand_spans};\\n00039| use write_program_request::{requested_write_program_parameters, write_program_parameters};\\n00040| \\n00041| #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\\n00042| pub enum IntentKind {\\n00043|     Task,\\n00044|     Question,\\n00045|     Requirement,\\n00046|     Statement,\\n00047|     Courtesy,\\n00048|     Unknown,\\n00049| }\\n00050| \\n00051| impl IntentKind {\\n00052|     #[must_use]\\n00053|     pub const fn slug(self) -> &'static str {\\n00054|         match self {\\n00055|             Self::Task => \\\"task\\\",\\n00056|             Self::Question => \\\"question\\\",\\n00057|             Self::Requirement => \\\"requirement\\\",\\n00058|             Self::Statement => \\\"statement\\\",\\n00059|             Self::Courtesy => \\\"courtesy\\\",\\n00060|             Self::Unknown => \\\"unknown\\\",\\n00061|         }\\n00062|     }\\n00063| }\\n00064| \\n00065| #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\\n00066| pub struct IntentFormalization {\\n00067|     pub impulse_id: String,\\n00068|     pub source_text: String,\\n00069|     pub normalized_text: String,\\n00070|     pub language: String,\\n00071|     pub kind: IntentKind,\\n00072|     pub knowns: Vec<String>,\\n00073|     pub relevants: Vec<String>,\\n00074|     pub parameters: BTreeMap<String, String>,\\n00075|     pub route: Option<String>,\\n00076|     pub response_link: Option<String>,\\n00077| }\\n00078| \\n00079| impl IntentFormalization {\\n00080|     /// Render this record with the shared Links Notation encoder so quoted\\n00081|     /// prompt text follows the same escaping rules as every other record.\\n00082|     #[must_use]\\n00083|     pub fn to_links_notation(&self) -> String {\\n00084|         let mut pairs = vec![\\n00085|             (\\\"impulse_id\\\", self.impulse_id.clone()),\\n00086|             (\\\"source_text\\\", self.source_text.clone()),\\n00087|             (\\\"normalized_text\\\", self.normalized_text.clone()),\\n00088|             (\\\"language\\\", self.language.clone()),\\n00089|             (\\\"kind\\\", self.kind.slug().to_owned()),\\n00090|         ];\\n00091|         if let Some(route) = &self.route {\\n00092|             pairs.push((\\\"route\\\", route.clone()));\\n00093|         }\\n00094|         if let Some(response_link) = &self.response_link {\\n00095|             pairs.push((\\\"response_link\\\", response_link.clone()));\\n00096|         }\\n00097|         for (name, value) in &self.parameters {\\n00098|             // `name=value` is one reference, so it is escaped once as a whole.\\n00099|             // Escaping the halves separately would put delimiters *inside* the\\n00100|             // reference and end it at the `=`.\\n00101|             pairs.push((\\\"parameter\\\", format!(\\\"{name}={value}\\\")));\\n00102|         }\\n00103|         for known in &self.knowns {\\n00104|             pairs.push((\\\"known\\\", known.clone()));\\n00105|         }\\n00106|         for relevant in &self.relevants {\\n00107|             pairs.push((\\\"relevant\\\", relevant.clone()));\\n00108|         }\\n00109| \\n00110|         // The header names the impulse the record is about, so it is a two-token\\n00111|         // link rather than a bare id; `format_lino_record` writes the id line\\n00112|         // verbatim, so that token is escaped here.\\n00113|         let mut out = format_lino_record(\\n00114|             &format!(\\n00115|                 \\\"intent_formalization {}\\\",\\n00116|                 escape_reference(&self.impulse_id)\\n00117|             ),\\n00118|             &pairs,\\n00119|         );\\n00120|         out.push('\\\\n');\\n00121|         out\\n00122|     }\\n00123| \\n00124|     #[must_use]\\n00125|     pub fn has_relevant_handler(&self, name: &str) -> bool {\\n00126|         self.relevants\\n00127|             .iter()\\n00128|             .any(|relevant| relevant == &format!(\\\"handler:{name}\\\"))\\n00129|     }\\n00130| }\\n00131| \\n00132| #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\\n00133| pub struct IntentFormalizationCacheEntry {\\n00134|     pub formalization: IntentFormalization,\\n00135|     pub cache_hit: bool,\\n00136| }\\n00137| \\n00138| #[derive(Debug, Default, Clone)]\\n00139| pub struct IntentFormalizationCache {\\n00140|     records: BTreeMap<String, IntentFormalization>,\\n00141| }\\n00142| \\n00143| impl IntentFormalizationCache {\\n00144|     #[must_use]\\n00145|     pub const fn new() -> Self {\\n00146|         Self {\\n00147|             records: BTreeMap::new(),\\n00148|         }\\n00149|     }\\n00150| \\n00151|     #[must_use]\\n00152|     pub fn len(&self) -> usize {\\n00153|         self.records.len()\\n00154|     }\\n00155| \\n00156|     #[must_use]\\n00157|     pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {\\n00158|         self.records.is_empty()\\n00159|     }\\n00160| \\n00161|     /// The cached record of this request, if one was formalized.\\n00162|     ///\\n00163|     /// Issue #1487: the impulse id is keyed on the normalized prompt, which\\n00164|     /// drops operators and punctuation, so `a < b` and `a <= b` (or `a - b`\\n00165|     /// and `a + b`) share one id. Sharing an id is retrieval, not identity: a\\n00166|     /// cached record answers only the request it was formalized from.\\n00167|     #[must_use]\\n00168|     pub fn get(&self, prompt: &str) -> Option<&IntentFormalization> {\\n00169|         let id = impulse_id_for(prompt);\\n00170|         self.records\\n00171|             .get(&id)\\n00172|             .filter(|formalization| same_request(&formalization.source_text, prompt))\\n00173|     }\\n00174| \\n00175|     pub fn formalize_or_insert(\\n00176|         &mut self,\\n00177|         prompt: &str,\\n00178|         language: &str,\\n00179|         candidate: Option<&FormalizationCandidate>,\\n00180|     ) -> IntentFormalizationCacheEntry {\\n00181|         if let Some(formalization) = self.get(prompt) {\\n00182|             return IntentFormalizationCacheEntry {\\n00183|                 formalization: formalization.clone(),\\n00184|                 cache_hit: true,\\n00185|             };\\n00186|         }\\n00187| \\n00188|         // A different request under the same impulse id keeps the first\\n00189|         // record; this one is formalized afresh every time (issue #1487).\\n00190|         let formalization = formalize_intent(prompt, language, candidate);\\n00191|         self.records\\n00192|             .entry(impulse_id_for(prompt))\\n00193|             .or_insert_with(|| formalization.clone());\\n00194|         IntentFormalizationCacheEntry {\\n00195|             formalization,\\n00196|             cache_hit: false,\\n00197|         }\\n00198|     }\\n00199| \\n00200|     pub fn append_to_link_store<S: LinkStore>(\\n00201|         &self,\\n00202|         store: &mut S,\\n00203|     ) -> Result<usize, LinkStoreError> {\\n00204|         let mut inserted = 0;\\n00205|         for formalization in self.records.values() {\\n00206|             store.append_memory_event(MemoryEvent {\\n00207|                 id: formalization.impulse_id.clone(),\\n00208|                 kind: Some(String::from(\\\"intent_formalization\\\")),\\n00209|                 content: Some(formalization.to_links_notation()),\\n00210|                 evidence: vec![format!(\\\"intent_formalization:{}\\\", formalization.impulse_id)],\\n00211|                 ..MemoryEvent::default()\\n00212|             })?;\\n00213|             inserted += 1;\\n00214|         }\\n00215|         Ok(inserted)\\n00216|     }\\n00217| }\\n00218| \\n00219| impl UniversalSolver {\\n00220|     #[must_use]\\n00221|     pub fn solve_with_intent_cache(\\n00222|         &self,\\n00223|         prompt: &str,\\n00224|         intent_cache: &mut IntentFormalizationCache,\\n00225|     ) -> crate::engine::SymbolicAnswer {\\n00226|         self.solve_with_history_probability_store_and_intent_cache(\\n00227|             prompt,\\n00228|             &[],\\n00229|             &ProbabilityStore::new(),\\n00230|             intent_cache,\\n00231|         )\\n00232|     }\\n00233| }\\n00234| \\n00235| #[must_use]\\n00236| pub fn impulse_id_for(prompt: &str) -> String {\\n00237|     stable_id(\\\"impulse\\\", &normalize_prompt(prompt))\\n00238| }\\n00239| \\n00240| /// Whether two prompts are the same request: equal up to whitespace. Case,\\n00241| /// operators, punctuation and quoted literals all stay significant (#1487).\\n00242| fn same_request(left: &str, right: &str) -> bool {\\n00243|     left.split_whitespace().eq(right.split_whitespace())\\n00244| }\\n00245| \\n00246| #[must_use]\\n00247| pub fn formalize_intent(\\n00248|     prompt: &str,\\n00249|     language: &str,\\n00250|     candidate: Option<&FormalizationCandidate>,\\n00251| ) -> IntentFormalization {\\n00252|     let normalized = normalize_prompt(prompt);\\n00253|     let route = route_for_prompt(prompt, &normalized);\\n00254|     let parameters = requested_write_program_parameters(prompt, &normalized).unwrap_or_default();\\n00255|     let mut knowns = vec![\\n00256|         format!(\\\"impulse:{}\\\", impulse_id_for(prompt)),\\n00257|         format!(\\\"language:{language}\\\"),\\n00258|     ];\\n00259|     let mut relevants = Vec::new();\\n00260| \\n00261|     if let Some(candidate) = candidate {\\n00262|         append_candidate_knowns(candidate, &mut knowns, &mut relevants);\\n00263|     }\\n00264|     for (name, value) in &parameters {\\n00265|         push_unique(&mut knowns, format!(\\\"parameter:{name}:{value}\\\"));\\n00266|     }\\n00267|     append_prompt_relevants(prompt, &normalized, &mut relevants);\\n00268|     for relevant in MethodRegistry::shared().explicit_learned_method_relevants(prompt) {\\n00269|         push_unique(&mut relevants, relevant);\\n00270|     }\\n00271| \\n00272|     let route_slug = route\\n00273|         .as_ref()\\n00274|         .map(|matched| matched.slug.clone())\\n00275|         .or_else(|| route_from_relevants(&relevants));\\n00276|     if let Some(route_slug) = &route_slug {\\n00277|         push_unique(&mut relevants, format!(\\\"route:{route_slug}\\\"));\\n00278|         if MethodRegistry::shared()\\n00279|             .method_for_route(route_slug)\\n00280|             .is_some()\\n00281|         {\\n00282|             push_unique(&mut relevants, format!(\\\"handler:{route_slug}\\\"));\\n00283|         }\\n00284|     }\\n00285| \\n00286|     IntentFormalization {\\n00287|         impulse_id: impulse_id_for(prompt),\\n00288|         source_text: prompt.to_owned(),\\n00289|         normalized_text: normalized.clone(),\\n00290|         language: language.to_owned(),\\n00291|         kind: infer_kind(prompt, &normalized, route_slug.as_deref(), candidate),\\n00292|         knowns,\\n00293|         relevants,\\n00294|         parameters,\\n00295|         response_link: route.map(|matched| matched.response_link),\\n00296|         route: route_slug,\\n00297|     }\\n00298| }\\n00299| \\n00300| pub(crate) fn record_intent_formalization(\\n00301|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00302|     entry: &IntentFormalizationCacheEntry,\\n00303| ) {\\n00304|     let formalization = &entry.formalization;\\n00305|     let cache_state = if entry.cache_hit { \\\"hit\\\" } else { \\\"miss\\\" };\\n00306|     log.append(\\n00307|         \\\"intent_formalization_cache\\\",\\n00308|         format!(\\\"{cache_state} {}\\\", formalization.impulse_id),\\n00309|     );\\n00310|     if entry.cache_hit {\\n00311|         log.append(\\n00312|             \\\"cache_hit\\\",\\n00313|             format!(\\\"intent_formalization:{}\\\", formalization.impulse_id),\\n00314|         );\\n00315|     } else {\\n00316|         log.append(\\\"intent_formalization\\\", formalization.to_links_notation());\\n00317|     }\\n00318|     log.append(\\n00319|         \\\"intent_formalization:kind\\\",\\n00320|         formalization.kind.slug().to_owned(),\\n00321|     );\\n00322|     if let Some(route) = &formalization.route {\\n00323|         log.append(\\\"intent_formalization:route\\\", route.clone());\\n00324|     }\\n00325|     for relevant in &formalization.relevants {\\n00326|         log.append(\\\"intent_formalization:relevant\\\", relevant.clone());\\n00327|     }\\n00328| }\\n00329| \\n00330| #[must_use]\\n00331| pub(crate) fn select_rule_for_intent(intent: &IntentFormalization) -> SelectedRule {\\n00332|     match intent.route.as_deref() {\\n00333|         Some(\\\"greeting\\\") => SelectedRule::Greeting,\\n00334|         Some(\\\"wellbeing\\\") => SelectedRule::Wellbeing,\\n00335|         Some(\\\"farewell\\\") => SelectedRule::Farewell,\\n00336|         Some(\\\"test_status\\\") => SelectedRule::TestStatus,\\n00337|         Some(\\\"courtesy_response\\\") => SelectedRule::CourtesyResponse,\\n00338|         Some(\\\"assistant_free_time\\\") => SelectedRule::AssistantFreeTime,\\n00339|         Some(\\\"assistant_name\\\") => SelectedRule::AssistantName,\\n00340|         Some(\\\"identity\\\") => SelectedRule::Identity,\\n00341|         Some(WRITE_PROGRAM_INTENT) => write_program_rule_for_intent(intent),\\n00342|         _ => SelectedRule::Unknown,\\n00343|     }\\n00344| }\\n00345| \\n00346| #[derive(Debug, Clone)]\\n00347| struct MatchedRoute {\\n00348|     slug: String,\\n00349|     response_link: String,\\n00350| }\\n00351| \\n00352| fn route_for_prompt(raw: &str, normalized: &str) -> Option<MatchedRoute> {\\n00353|     if requested_write_program_parameters(raw, normalized).is_some() {\\n00354|         return Some(MatchedRoute {\\n00355|             slug: String::from(WRITE_PROGRAM_INTENT),\\n00356|             response_link: String::from(\\\"response:write_program\\\"),\\n00357|         });\\n00358|     }\\n00359|     // A pasted `def` under a review, explanation, refactoring or debugging\\n00360|     // instruction is the code that instruction acts on, not a task to\\n00361|     // synthesize (issue #1177), so it does not promote program synthesis.\\n00362|     if crate::coding::task_spec::recognise(raw).is_some()\\n00363|         && !crate::solver_dispatch::code_artifact_task_claims(raw)\\n00364|     {\\n00365|         return Some(MatchedRoute {\\n00366|             slug: String::from(\\\"program_synthesis\\\"),\\n00367|             response_link: String::from(\\\"response:write_program:synthesis\\\"),\\n00368|         });\\n00369|     }\\n00370|     // Before the table: the conversational families' blocks sat ahead of the\\n00371|     // write_program family's, so their rows claimed `hello` before\\n00372|     // write_program's `keyword hello` (a row shadowed since it was written).\\n00373|     // The declared role surfaces keep that precedence for the retired rows.\\n00374|     declared_role_surface_route(normalized).or_else(|| {\\n00375|         seed::intent_routing()\\n00376|             .intents\\n00377|             .iter()\\n00378|             .find(|route| {\\n00379|                 matches_route(normalized, route)\\n00380|                     && !(route.slug == \\\"identity\\\"\\n00381|                         && crate::formalization::dialogue::blocks_self_description(\\n00382|                             raw,\\n00383|                             crate::language::detect(raw).slug(),\\n00384|                         ))\\n00385|             })\\n00386|             .map(|route| MatchedRoute {\\n00387|                 slug: route.slug.clone(),\\n00388|                 response_link: route.response_link.clone(),\\n00389|             })\\n00390|     })\\n00391| }\\n00392| \\n00393| /// The conversational families' bare whole prompts (`hi`, `how are you`,\\n00394| /// `who are you`) retired onto seeded roles (issue #1138 plan 10 leaf 20):\\n00395| /// the prompt has no object to derive from, so the decision is the declared\\n00396| /// roles' surface inventories under the same whole-prompt equality the\\n00397| /// exact-match rows had. The declaration lives on the family's own block\\n00398| /// (`role_surface <role>`), so a future family retires its rows by seeding\\n00399| /// the role and declaring it — no code change. Compound courtesy\\n00400| /// (`привет как дела`) is a surface of its own family's role, exactly as it\\n00401| /// was a phrase row.\\n00402| fn declared_role_surface_route(normalized: &str) -> Option<MatchedRoute> {\\n00403|     seed::intent_routing()\\n00404|         .intents\\n00405|         .iter()\\n00406|         .filter(|route| !route.role_surfaces.is_empty())\\n00407|         .find(|route| {\\n00408|             route.role_surfaces.iter().any(|role| {\\n00409|                 seed::lexicon()\\n00410|                     .words_for_role(role)\\n00411|                     .iter()\\n00412|                     .any(|word| word.as_str() == normalized)\\n00413|             })\\n00414|         })\\n00415|         .map(|route| MatchedRoute {\\n00416|             slug: route.slug.clone(),\\n00417|             response_link: route.response_link.clone(),\\n00418|         })\\n00419| }\\n00420| \\n00421| fn matches_route(normalized: &str, route: &seed::IntentRoute) -> bool {\\n00422|     route.keywords.iter().any(|keyword| normalized == keyword)\\n00423|         || route.phrases.iter().any(|phrase| normalized == phrase)\\n00424|         || route\\n00425|             .tokens\\n00426|             .iter()\\n00427|             .any(|token| contains_token(normalized, token))\\n00428|         || route.combos.iter().any(|combo| {\\n00429|             !combo.is_empty() && combo.iter().all(|token| contains_token(normalized, token))\\n00430|         })\\n00431| }\\n00432| \\n00433| fn contains_token(normalized: &str, expected: &str) -> bool {\\n00434|     // CJK scripts have no inter-word spaces, so match those aliases by substring\\n00435|     // (see `coding::catalog::contains_cjk`). Latin/Cyrillic keep strict\\n00436|     // whitespace boundaries so short tokens never match inside larger words.\\n00437|     if crate::coding::contains_cjk(expected) {\\n00438|         return normalized.contains(expected);\\n00439|     }\\n00440|     normalized.split_whitespace().any(|token| token =[REDACTED] expected)\\n00441| }\\n00442| \\n00443| fn write_program_rule_for_intent(intent: &IntentFormalization) -> SelectedRule {\\n00444|     let task = intent.parameters.get(\\\"task\\\").cloned();\\n00445|     let language = intent.parameters.get(\\\"language\\\").cloned();\\n00446|     if let (Some(task_slug), Some(language_slug)) = (task.as_deref(), language.as_deref())\\n00447|         && let Some(spec) = program_spec(task_slug, language_slug)\\n00448|     {\\n00449|         return SelectedRule::WriteProgram(spec);\\n00450|     }\\n00451|     SelectedRule::UnsupportedWriteProgram { task, language }\\n00452| }\\n00453| \\n00454| /// Outcome of trying to complete a follow-up `write_program` request from the\\n00455| /// conversation so far (issue #324).\\n00456| pub(crate) struct WriteProgramRecovery {\\n00457|     /// The rule after recovery — upgraded to [`SelectedRule::WriteProgram`] when\\n00458|     /// enough context was found, otherwise the original unsupported rule with any\\n00459|     /// parameters we managed to fill in.\\n00460|     pub rule: SelectedRule,\\n00461|     /// A short trace describing what was carried over, for the event log. `None`\\n00462|     /// when nothing was recovered.\\n00463|     pub trace: Option<String>,\\n00464|     /// The program-modification plan as Links Notation, surfaced when a modifier\\n00465|     /// rewrote the task via the substitution pipeline (issue #324 R4/R6). `None`\\n00466|     /// when no modification rule fired.\\n00467|     pub plan: Option<String>,\\n00468| }\\n00469| \\n00470| /// Outcome of recognizing a bare imperative as a follow-up that refers to the\\n00471| /// active program artifact rather than a standalone algorithm/text request.\\n00472| pub(crate) struct ProgramCoreferenceRewrite {\\n00473|     pub rule: SelectedRule,\\n00474|     pub trace: String,\\n00475| }\\n00476| \\n00477| /// Issue #357: after a program has been generated, users often ask bare\\n00478| /// imperative follow-ups such as \\\"Sort the results in reverse order\\\" or\\n00479| /// \\\"Сделай сортировку результатов...\\\" without repeating \\\"program\\\". Those turns\\n00480| /// may route to another handler (or to unknown) even though \\\"results\\\" refers to\\n00481| /// the active program artifact. Reclassify only that narrow shape as an\\n00482| /// unsupported `write_program` request, then let the existing context recovery\\n00483| /// bind the concrete task and language.\\n00484| #[must_use]\\n00485| pub(crate) fn rewrite_bare_program_coreference_rule(\\n00486|     rule: &SelectedRule,\\n00487|     follow_up: &str,\\n00488|     history: &[ConversationTurn],\\n00489| ) -> Option<ProgramCoreferenceRewrite> {\\n00490|     if matches!(\\n00491|         rule,\\n00492|         SelectedRule::WriteProgram(_) | SelectedRule::UnsupportedWriteProgram { .. }\\n00493|     ) {\\n00494|         return None;\\n00495|     }\\n00496| \\n00497|     let normalized = normalize_prompt(follow_up);\\n00498|     if !crate::program_coreference::looks_like_bare_program_artifact_follow_up(&normalized) {\\n00499|         return None;\\n00500|     }\\n00501| \\n00502|     let context = active_program_context(history)?;\\n00503|     let trace = format!(\\n00504|         \\\"referent=active_program_artifact task={} language={}\\\",\\n00505|         context.task, context.language\\n00506|     );\\n00507|     Some(ProgramCoreferenceRewrite {\\n00508|         rule: SelectedRule::UnsupportedWriteProgram {\\n00509|             task: Some(context.task),\\n00510|             language: Some(context.language),\\n00511|         },\\n00512|         trace,\\n00513|     })\\n00514| }\\n00515| \\n00516| pub(crate) struct ActiveProgramContext {\\n00517|     pub(crate) task: String,\\n00518|     pub(crate) language: String,\\n00519| }\\n00520| \\n00521| pub(crate) fn active_program_context(history: &[ConversationTurn]) -> Option<ActiveProgramContext> {\\n00522|     let mut task = None;\\n00523|     let mut language = None;\\n00524|     for turn in history.iter().rev() {\\n00525|         let normalized = normalize_prompt(&turn.content);\\n00526|         let Some(parameters) = write_program_parameters(&normalized) else {\\n00527|             continue;\\n00528|         };\\n00529|         if task.is_none() {\\n00530|             task = parameters.get(\\\"task\\\").cloned();\\n00531|         }\\n00532|         if language.is_none() {\\n00533|             language = parameters.get(\\\"language\\\").cloned();\\n00534|         }\\n00535|         if task.is_some() && language.is_some() {\\n00536|             break;\\n00537|         }\\n00538|     }\\n00539|     Some(ActiveProgramContext {\\n00540|         task: task?,\\n00541|         language: language?,\\n00542|     })\\n00543| }\\n00544| \\n00545| /// Issue #324: a follow-up such as \\\"Сделай так, чтобы программа принимала путь\\n00546| /// как аргумент\\\" (\\\"make the program accept a path as an argument\\\") routes to\\n00547| /// `write_program` because it pairs a program noun with an imperative verb, yet\\n00548| /// it names neither a concrete task nor a language — both came from the previous\\n00549| /// turn. Without conversation context this surfaced the user-reported error\\n00550| /// (\\\"I do not have a template for language `missing` and task `missing`\\\").\\n00551| ///\\n00552| /// When the selected rule is [`SelectedRule::UnsupportedWriteProgram`] we recover\\n00553| /// the missing task and language from the most recent prior turn that named them\\n00554| /// and apply any data-defined modification modifier present in the follow-up.\\n00555| /// If the recovered `(task, language)` pair has a template we upgrade the rule\\n00556| /// to a concrete program; otherwise we return the rule with whatever we could\\n00557| /// fill in so the unsupported message is still as specific as possible.\\n00558| #[must_use]\\n00559| pub(crate) fn recover_write_program_rule(\\n00560|     rule: SelectedRule,\\n00561|     follow_up: &str,\\n00562|     history: &[ConversationTurn],\\n00563| ) -> WriteProgramRecovery {\\n00564|     let SelectedRule::UnsupportedWriteProgram { task, language } = &rule else {\\n00565|         return WriteProgramRecovery {\\n00566|             rule,\\n00567|             trace: None,\\n00568|             plan: None,\\n00569|         };\\n00570|     };\\n00571| \\n00572|     let mut recovered_task = task.clone();\\n00573|     let mut recovered_language = language.clone();\\n00574| \\n00575|     if recovered_task.is_none() || recovered_language.is_none() {\\n00576|         for turn in history.iter().rev() {\\n00577|             let normalized = normalize_prompt(&turn.content);\\n00578|             let Some(parameters) = write_program_parameters(&normalized) else {\\n00579|                 continue;\\n00580|             };\\n00581|             if recovered_task.is_none() {\\n00582|                 recovered_task = parameters.get(\\\"task\\\").cloned();\\n00583|             }\\n00584|             if recovered_language.is_none() {\\n00585|                 recovered_language = parameters.get(\\\"language\\\").cloned();\\n00586|             }\\n00587|             if recovered_task.is_some() && recovered_language.is_some() {\\n00588|                 break;\\n00589|             }\\n00590|         }\\n00591|     }\\n00592| \\n00593|     // A modification follow-up lowers the recovered base task through the Links\\n00594|     // Notation substitution pipeline, which rewrites e.g. `list_files ->\\n00595|     // list_files_arg` or `list_files_arg -> list_files_arg_reverse_sort`. The\\n00596|     // plan is captured as Links Notation for transparent tracing.\\n00597|     let normalized_follow_up = normalize_prompt(follow_up);\\n00598|     let modifiers = detected_program_modifiers(&normalized_follow_up);\\n00599|     let mut plan = None;\\n00600|     if !modifiers.is_empty()\\n00601|         && let Some(base) = recovered_task.as_deref()\\n00602|     {\\n00603|         let lowered = crate::program_plan::lower(base, &modifiers);\\n00604|         if lowered.was_modified() {\\n00605|             plan = Some(lowered.links_notation());\\n00606|         }\\n00607|         recovered_task = Some(lowered.resolved_task);\\n00608|     }\\n00609| \\n00610|     if let (Some(task_slug), Some(language_slug)) =\\n00611|         (recovered_task.as_deref(), recovered_language.as_deref())\\n00612|         && let Some(spec) = program_spec(task_slug, language_slug)\\n00613|     {\\n00614|         let trace = format!(\\\"write_program task={task_slug} language={language_slug}\\\");\\n00615|         return WriteProgramRecovery {\\n00616|             rule: SelectedRule::WriteProgram(spec),\\n00617|             trace: Some(trace),\\n00618|             plan,\\n00619|         };\\n00620|     }\\n00621| \\n00622|     WriteProgramRecovery {\\n00623|         rule: SelectedRule::UnsupportedWriteProgram {\\n00624|             task: recovered_task,\\n00625|             language: recovered_language,\\n00626|         },\\n00627|         trace: None,\\n00628|         plan,\\n00629|     }\\n00630| }\\n00631| \\n00632| fn operation_vocabulary() -> &'static seed::OperationVocabulary {\\n00633|     static VOCABULARY: OnceLock<seed::OperationVocabulary> = OnceLock::new();\\n00634|     VOCABULARY.get_or_init(seed::operation_vocabulary)\\n00635| }\\n00636| \\n00637| /// Detect the modification modifiers present in a (normalized) request, returned\\n00638| /// as the slugs the substitution pipeline keys on.\\n00639| ///\\n00640| /// Recognition is data-driven in two stages: `operation-vocabulary.lino` owns\\n00641| /// natural-language trigger phrases, and `program-plan-rules.lino` decides which\\n00642| /// operation slugs are valid program modifiers by declaring\\n00643| /// `request:modifier -> <slug>` conditions.\\n00644| pub(crate) fn detected_program_modifiers(normalized: &str) -> Vec<String> {\\n00645|     let program_modifiers = crate::program_plan::modifier_slugs();\\n00646|     operation_vocabulary()\\n00647|         .detect(normalized)\\n00648|         .into_iter()\\n00649|         .filter(|slug| program_modifiers.contains(slug.as_str()))\\n00650|         .collect()\\n00651| }\\n00652| \\n00653| fn append_candidate_knowns(\\n00654|     candidate: &FormalizationCandidate,\\n00655|     knowns: &mut Vec<String>,\\n00656|     relevants: &mut Vec<String>,\\n00657| ) {\\n00658|     for slot in &candidate.slots {\\n00659|         push_unique(\\n00660|             knowns,\\n00661|             slot_known_link(slot.role, slot.anchor.kind, &slot.anchor.id),\\n00662|         );\\n00663|         if slot.role == FormalizationRole::Predicate && slot.anchor.id == \\\"wikidata:P5972\\\" {\\n00664|             push_unique(relevants, String::from(\\\"handler:translation\\\"));\\n00665|             push_unique(relevants, String::from(\\\"route:translation\\\"));\\n00666|         }\\n00667|     }\\n00668|     for term in &candidate.unresolved_terms {\\n00669|         push_unique(knowns, format!(\\\"formalization_unresolved:{term}\\\"));\\n00670|     }\\n00671| }\\n00672| \\n00673| fn slot_known_link(role: FormalizationRole, kind: FormalizationAnchorKind, id: &str) -> String {\\n00674|     match (role, kind) {\\n00675|         (FormalizationRole::Subject, FormalizationAnchorKind::WikidataItem) => {\\n00676|             format!(\\\"formalization:subject_q:{id}\\\")\\n00677|         }\\n00678|         (FormalizationRole::Predicate, FormalizationAnchorKind::WikidataProperty) => {\\n00679|             format!(\\\"formalization:predicate_p:{id}\\\")\\n00680|         }\\n00681|         (FormalizationRole::Object, FormalizationAnchorKind::WikidataItem) => {\\n00682|             format!(\\\"formalization:object_q:{id}\\\")\\n00683|         }\\n00684|         (_, FormalizationAnchorKind::WikidataItem) => {\\n00685|             format!(\\\"formalization:item_q:{id}\\\")\\n00686|         }\\n00687|         (_, FormalizationAnchorKind::WikidataProperty) => {\\n00688|             format!(\\\"formalization:property_p:{id}\\\")\\n00689|         }\\n00690|         (\\n00691|             _,\\n00692|             FormalizationAnchorKind::WikipediaArticle | FormalizationAnchorKind::WiktionaryEntry,\\n00693|         ) => {\\n00694|             format!(\\\"formalization:fallback:{id}\\\")\\n00695|         }\\n00696|         (_, FormalizationAnchorKind::RawText) => format!(\\\"formalization:raw:{id}\\\"),\\n00697|     }\\n00698| }\\n00699| \\n00700| fn has_any_token(normalized: &str, tokens: &[&str]) -> bool {\\n00701|     tokens.iter().any(|token| contains_token(normalized, token))\\n00702| }\\n00703| \\n00704| fn route_from_relevants(relevants: &[String]) -> Option<String> {\\n00705|     let registry = MethodRegistry::shared();\\n00706|     relevants.iter().find_map(|relevant| {\\n00707|         let slug = relevant\\n00708|             .strip_prefix(\\\"route:\\\")\\n00709|             .or_else(|| relevant.strip_prefix(\\\"handler:\\\"))\\n00710|             .filter(|slug| registry.method_for_route(slug).is_some())?;\\n00711|         Some(slug.to_owned())\\n00712|     })\\n00713| }\\n00714| \\n00715| fn infer_kind(\\n00716|     prompt: &str,\\n00717|     normalized: &str,\\n00718|     route: Option<&str>,\\n00719|     candidate: Option<&FormalizationCandidate>,\\n00720| ) -> IntentKind {\\n00721|     match route {\\n00722|         Some(\\\"greeting\\\" | \\\"wellbeing\\\" | \\\"farewell\\\" | \\\"courtesy_response\\\") => IntentKind::Courtesy,\\n00723|         Some(\\\"assistant_name\\\" | \\\"identity\\\") => IntentKind::Question,\\n00724|         Some(\\n00725|             \\\"translation\\\"\\n00726|             | \\\"algorithm\\\"\\n00727|             | \\\"write_program\\\"\\n00728|             | \\\"program_synthesis\\\"\\n00729|             | \\\"text_manipulation\\\"\\n00730|             | \\\"software_project_plan\\\"\\n00731|             | \\\"software_project_implementation\\\",\\n00732|         ) => IntentKind::Task,\\n00733|         _ if contains_question_mark(prompt) || starts_with_question_word(normalized) => {\\n00734|             IntentKind::Question\\n00735|         }\\n00736|         _ if has_any_token(normalized, &[\\\"must\\\", \\\"should\\\", \\\"require\\\", \\\"requires\\\"]) => {\\n00737|             IntentKind::Requirement\\n00738|         }\\n00739|         _ if has_any_token(\\n00740|             normalized,\\n00741|             &[\\n00742|                 \\\"translate\\\",\\n00743|                 \\\"write\\\",\\n00744|                 \\\"calculate\\\",\\n00745|                 \\\"search\\\",\\n00746|                 \\\"find\\\",\\n00747|                 \\\"prove\\\",\\n00748|                 \\\"define\\\",\\n00749|             ],\\n00750|         ) =>\\n00751|         {\\n00752|             IntentKind::Task\\n00753|         }\\n00754|         _ if candidate.is_some_and(|candidate| !candidate.slots.is_empty()) => {\\n00755|             IntentKind::Statement\\n00756|         }\\n00757|         _ => IntentKind::Unknown,\\n00758|     }\\n00759| }\\n00760| \\n00761| pub(crate) fn starts_with_question_word(normalized: &str) -> bool {\\n00762|     // The interrogative openers (the wh-words) are carried by the\\n00763|     // `interrogative_opener` meaning in the seed, not hardcoded here. English and\\n00764|     // Russian are head-initial, so the opener fronts the prompt and a prefix match\\n00765|     // — the bare word followed immediately by a space — detects it; the head-final\\n00766|     // Hindi and Chinese surfaces are carried for coverage but place the question\\n00767|     // word later, which this front scan does not chase.\\n00768|     crate::seed::lexicon()\\n00769|         .words_for_role_in_languages(crate::seed::ROLE_INTERROGATIVE_OPENER, &[\\\"en\\\", \\\"ru\\\"])\\n00770|         .iter()\\n00771|         .any(|word| {\\n00772|             normalized\\n00773|                 .strip_prefix(word.as_str())\\n00774|                 .is_some_and(|rest| rest.starts_with(' '))\\n00775|         })\\n00776| }\\n00777| \\n00778| pub(crate) fn contains_question_mark(prompt: &str) -> bool {\\n00779|     prompt.contains('?') || prompt.contains('？')\\n00780| }\\n00781| \\n00782| fn push_unique(values: &mut Vec<String>, value: String) {\\n00783|     if !values.contains(&value) {\\n00784|         values.push(value);\\n00785|     }\\n00786| }\\n\\n(End of file - total 786 lines)\\n</file>\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794915285086/rust/src/solver_unknown_reasoning.rs and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_f5a28fb5cc4cbd34\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794915285086/rust/src/solver_unknown_reasoning.rs\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_f5a28fb5cc4cbd34\",\"content\":\"<file>\\n00001| //! Unknown-prompt reasoning path.\\n00002| //!\\n00003| //! This module handles the point where every known rule and specialized\\n00004| //! handler has declined a prompt. It still records a bounded reasoning trace,\\n00005| //! tries reachable link-memory and public-cache sources, and only then returns\\n00006| //! an unknown answer.\\n00007| \\n00008| use std::sync::OnceLock;\\n00009| \\n00010| use crate::concept_lookup::ConceptSense;\\n00011| use crate::concepts::{ConceptQuery, lookup_concept_query};\\n00012| use crate::engine::{SymbolicAnswer, normalize_prompt, stable_id};\\n00013| use crate::event_log::EventLog;\\n00014| use crate::language::Language;\\n00015| use crate::seed::{self, ConceptRecord, localized_response};\\n00016| use crate::solver_handlers::{WebSearchQueryKind, answer_web_search_query, finalize_simple};\\n00017| use crate::solver_helpers::{humanize_url, miss_names_consulted_sources};\\n00018| use crate::unknown_opener::language_aware_unknown_answer;\\n00019| \\n00020| const FOCUS_PLACEHOLDER: &str = concat!(\\\"{\\\", \\\"focus\\\", \\\"}\\\");\\n00021| \\n00022| #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\\n00023| pub struct UnknownReasoningConfig {\\n00024|     pub questioning_rigor: f32,\\n00025|     pub offline: bool,\\n00026|     pub agent_mode: bool,\\n00027| }\\n00028| \\n00029| pub fn answer_unknown_prompt(\\n00030|     prompt: &str,\\n00031|     language: Language,\\n00032|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00033|     config: UnknownReasoningConfig,\\n00034|     retrieved_senses: &[ConceptSense],\\n00035| ) -> SymbolicAnswer {\\n00036|     let focus = infer_missing_focus(prompt);\\n00037|     record_initial_unknown_trace(prompt, language, log, focus.as_deref(), config);\\n00038| \\n00039|     log.append(\\\"reasoning:candidate_source\\\", \\\"link_memory\\\".to_owned());\\n00040|     log.append(\\n00041|         \\\"reasoning:gather_attempt\\\",\\n00042|         focus.as_deref().map_or_else(\\n00043|             || \\\"link_memory:no_focus\\\".to_owned(),\\n00044|             |value| format!(\\\"link_memory:{value}\\\"),\\n00045|         ),\\n00046|     );\\n00047|     if let Some(focus) = focus.as_deref()\\n00048|         && let Some(body) = answer_from_link_memory(prompt, focus, log)\\n00049|     {\\n00050|         return finalize_simple(\\n00051|             prompt,\\n00052|             log,\\n00053|             \\\"memory_fact_lookup\\\",\\n00054|             \\\"response:memory_fact_lookup\\\",\\n00055|             &body,\\n00056|             0.85,\\n00057|         );\\n00058|     }\\n00059|     log.append(\\\"reasoning:gather_result\\\", \\\"link_memory:miss\\\".to_owned());\\n00060| \\n00061|     log.append(\\n00062|         \\\"reasoning:candidate_source\\\",\\n00063|         \\\"public_knowledge_cache\\\".to_owned(),\\n00064|     );\\n00065|     log.append(\\n00066|         \\\"reasoning:gather_attempt\\\",\\n00067|         focus.as_deref().map_or_else(\\n00068|             || \\\"public_knowledge_cache:no_focus\\\".to_owned(),\\n00069|             |value| format!(\\\"public_knowledge_cache:{value}\\\"),\\n00070|         ),\\n00071|     );\\n00072|     if let Some(focus) = focus.as_deref()\\n00073|         && let Some(answer) = answer_from_public_knowledge_cache(prompt, focus, language, log)\\n00074|     {\\n00075|         return answer;\\n00076|     }\\n00077|     log.append(\\n00078|         \\\"reasoning:gather_result\\\",\\n00079|         \\\"public_knowledge_cache:miss\\\".to_owned(),\\n00080|     );\\n00081| \\n00082|     if let Some(answer) = answer_from_retrieved_senses(prompt, language, log, retrieved_senses) {\\n00083|         return answer;\\n00084|     }\\n00085|     if let Some(answer) = answer_from_concept_lookup_miss(prompt, language, log, focus.as_deref()) {\\n00086|         return answer;\\n00087|     }\\n00088| \\n00089|     let language_supported = seed::supported_languages()\\n00090|         .iter()\\n00091|         .any(|supported| supported == language.slug());\\n00092|     // Wave F (issue #1138, plan 01): when the consult walk has already asked\\n00093|     // the sources and recorded its misses, the web-search handoff below would\\n00094|     // answer for the very focus the walk just missed — the walk's own evidence\\n00095|     // must not be outranked by a second opinion on the same word. A question\\n00096|     // that turns on an unresolved word has already been looked up by the time\\n00097|     // this arm is reached. Two shapes escape: a bare term is itself a lookup\\n00098|     // request — the prompt is the term and nothing else — so the search is the\\n00099|     // answer in every language and the walk's verdict does not outrank it; and\\n00100|     // an instruction (issue #873, R873-1) names no single looked-up word for\\n00101|     // the walk's evidence to answer for, so an unresolved imperative stays a\\n00102|     // research trigger. The question shape is `?` in either width or a\\n00103|     // seed-carried interrogative opener fronting the prompt.\\n00104|     let consult_walk_ran = log.events().iter().any(|event| {\\n00105|         event.kind == \\\"concept_lookup:miss\\\" && miss_names_consulted_sources(&event.payload)\\n00106|     });\\n00107|     let question_shape = is_question_shape(prompt);\\n00108|     if let Some(focus) = focus.as_deref().filter(|focus| {\\n00109|         let bare_term_lookup = is_unresolved_bare_term_prompt(prompt, focus);\\n00110|         (!consult_walk_ran || bare_term_lookup || !question_shape)\\n00111|             && language_supported\\n00112|             && (!config.offline || (bare_term_lookup && focus_is_specific(prompt, focus)))\\n00113|     }) {\\n00114|         let kind = if is_unresolved_bare_term_prompt(prompt, focus) {\\n00115|             WebSearchQueryKind::UnresolvedBareTerm\\n00116|         } else {\\n00117|             WebSearchQueryKind::UnknownReasoningFallback\\n00118|         };\\n00119|         log.append(\\\"reasoning:candidate_source\\\", \\\"web_search\\\".to_owned());\\n00120|         log.append(\\\"reasoning:gather_attempt\\\", format!(\\\"web_search:{focus}\\\"));\\n00121|         // Issue #1173 (E138): this handoff used to return the canned\\n00122|         // description of the browser demo's search machinery (\\\"Web search\\n00123|         // requested for …\\\") as the final answer. It now executes the search\\n00124|         // for the focus through the process source cache: captured sources\\n00125|         // answer from their statements with citations, and an offline or\\n00126|         // cache-miss run degrades to the localized `web_search_unavailable`\\n00127|         // response that names the focus. Live provider fetches stay behind\\n00128|         // the `FORMAL_AI_LIVE_FETCH` opt-in, so the runtime's offline mode\\n00129|         // keeps its no-network boundary.\\n00130|         return answer_web_search_query(prompt, focus, kind, log, config.offline);\\n00131|     }\\n00132| \\n00133|     log.append(\\n00134|         \\\"reasoning:candidate_source\\\",\\n00135|         if config.offline {\\n00136|             \\\"allowed_external_api:skipped_offline\\\"\\n00137|         } else {\\n00138|             \\\"allowed_external_api:no_verified_value\\\"\\n00139|         }\\n00140|         .to_owned(),\\n00141|     );\\n00142| \\n00143|     // An unsupported language cannot nominate a narrower focus than the\\n00144|     // prompt itself, so the whole prompt is the researchable surface and the\\n00145|     // `_unknown` response templates carry the fall-back-to-English notice\\n00146|     // for it; the specificity filter stays for languages we can parse.\\n00147|     if let Some(focus) =\\n00148|         focus.filter(|focus| focus_is_specific(prompt, focus) || !language_supported)\\n00149|     {\\n00150|         return answer_unresolved_unknown(prompt, language, log, &focus, config);\\n00151|     }\\n00152| \\n00153|     answer_with_legacy_fallback(prompt, language, log, config)\\n00154| }\\n00155| \\n00156| fn answer_from_retrieved_senses(\\n00157|     prompt: &str,\\n00158|     language: Language,\\n00159|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00160|     senses: &[ConceptSense],\\n00161| ) -> Option<SymbolicAnswer> {\\n00162|     let sense = senses\\n00163|         .iter()\\n00164|         .enumerate()\\n00165|         .max_by_key(|(index, sense)| (sense.tier.weight_percent(), usize::MAX - index))?\\n00166|         .1;\\n00167|     let digest = sense.sha256.get(..16).unwrap_or(&sense.sha256);\\n00168|     let mut body = seed::render_response(\\n00169|         \\\"concept_lookup_resolved\\\",\\n00170|         language.slug(),\\n00171|         &[\\n00172|             (\\\"surface\\\", &sense.surface),\\n00173|             (\\\"lemma\\\", &sense.lemma),\\n00174|             (\\\"gloss\\\", &sense.gloss),\\n00175|             (\\\"source\\\", &sense.source_url),\\n00176|             (\\\"sha256\\\", digest),\\n00177|             (\\\"license\\\", &sense.license_name),\\n00178|         ],\\n00179|     )?;\\n00180|     // The answer names what was *not* consulted too: a source the settings\\n00181|     // opted out is reported beside the sense that was found without it, so the\\n00182|     // absence is attributable to the user's own choice (plan 01 failure\\n00183|     // behaviour, outcome 3).\\n00184|     for note in disabled_notes(log, language.slug()) {\\n00185|         body.push('\\\\n');\\n00186|         body.push_str(&note);\\n00187|     }\\n00188|     log.append(\\n00189|         \\\"reasoning:gather_result\\\",\\n00190|         format!(\\\"external_source:hit:{}\\\", sense.content_id()),\\n00191|     );\\n00192|     Some(finalize_simple(\\n00193|         prompt,\\n00194|         log,\\n00195|         \\\"concept_lookup\\\",\\n00196|         \\\"response:concept_lookup_resolved\\\",\\n00197|         &body,\\n00198|         f32::from(sense.tier.weight_percent()) / 100.0,\\n00199|     ))\\n00200| }\\n00201| \\n00202| /// The localized \\\"this source was opted out\\\" note for every disabled row the\\n00203| /// lookup recorded, deduplicated, so a resolved answer stays complete about\\n00204| /// what was skipped beside it.\\n00205| fn disabled_notes(log: &EventLog, language: &str) -> Vec<String> {\\n00206|     let mut notes: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\\n00207|     for event in log\\n00208|         .events()\\n00209|         .iter()\\n00210|         .filter(|event| event.kind == \\\"concept_lookup:disabled\\\")\\n00211|     {\\n00212|         let Some(source) = payload_field(&event.payload, \\\"source\\\") else {\\n00213|             continue;\\n00214|         };\\n00215|         let Some(settings_key) = payload_field(&event.payload, \\\"settings_key\\\") else {\\n00216|             continue;\\n00217|         };\\n00218|         let Some(note) = seed::render_response(\\n00219|             \\\"concept_lookup_disabled\\\",\\n00220|             language,\\n00221|             &[(\\\"source\\\", &source), (\\\"settings_key\\\", &settings_key)],\\n00222|         ) else {\\n00223|             continue;\\n00224|         };\\n00225|         if !notes.contains(&note) {\\n00226|             notes.push(note);\\n00227|         }\\n00228|     }\\n00229|     notes\\n00230| }\\n00231| \\n00232| /// Read one `key=value` field out of a structured trace payload.\\n00233| fn payload_field(payload: &str, name: &str) -> Option<String> {\\n00234|     payload.split(' ').find_map(|field| {\\n00235|         field\\n00236|             .split_once('=')\\n00237|             .filter(|(key, _)| *key == name)\\n00238|             .map(|(_, value)| value.to_owned())\\n00239|     })\\n00240| }\\n00241| \\n00242| /// Project an attributable concept miss instead of replacing it with generic\\n00243| /// web-search planning prose. `record_external_search` has already asked the\\n00244| /// registry and recorded every outcome; this function only turns that evidence\\n00245| /// into the localized response declared by the seed.\\n00246| fn answer_from_concept_lookup_miss(\\n00247|     prompt: &str,\\n00248|     language: Language,\\n00249|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00250|     focus: Option<&str>,\\n00251| ) -> Option<SymbolicAnswer> {\\n00252|     let consulted = log\\n00253|         .events()\\n00254|         .iter()\\n00255|         .filter(|event| event.kind == \\\"concept_lookup:miss\\\")\\n00256|         .filter_map(|event| {\\n00257|             event\\n00258|                 .payload\\n00259|                 .split_once(\\\"consulted=\\\")\\n00260|                 .map(|(_, value)| value)\\n00261|         })\\n00262|         .filter(|value| !value.trim().is_empty())\\n00263|         .collect::<Vec<_>>()\\n00264|         .join(\\\"; \\\");\\n00265|     if consulted.is_empty() {\\n00266|         return None;\\n00267|     }\\n00268|     let surface = focus.unwrap_or(prompt).trim();\\n00269|     // A whole-prompt bare term is the search handoff's turn (the escalation\\n00270|     // below presents the web-search plan for it); an in-sentence term keeps\\n00271|     // the consulted-source record here. A focus that covers the prompt's\\n00272|     // whole question subject is the same case with more words — unless the\\n00273|     // prompt is itself a definition question of that subject, where the\\n00274|     // record is the direct answer to what was asked. The distinction is the\\n00275|     // seeded definition-question recognizer: \\\"what does X mean\\\" owes the\\n00276|     // consulted-source record, while \\\"how should X be calibrated\\\" asks an\\n00277|     // operative question the record would only restate, so the unknown\\n00278|     // answer with its issue-report invitation owns the final answer and the\\n00279|     // consulted trail stays in the recorded events. A definition-fusion\\n00280|     // directive the merge arm already declined is the same operative shape\\n00281|     // with no question to hang the record on: the merge arm's own miss\\n00282|     // event marks it, and fabricating a definitional non-answer would\\n00283|     // stand where the honest unknown belongs.\\n00284|     let merge_arm_declined = log\\n00285|         .events()\\n00286|         .iter()\\n00287|         .any(|event| event.kind == \\\"definition_merge:miss\\\");\\n00288|     // A quoted-example question — «…» липограммой …, \\\"…\\\" a lipogram …,\\n00289|     // \\\"…\\\" लिपोग्राम … — turns on the unresolved predicate named outside the\\n00290|     // quotes, and the consult walk has already asked the sources for exactly\\n00291|     // that predicate (a quoted span is the question's subject, never a\\n00292|     // concept the system is missing, so every miss the walk recorded is that\\n00293|     // predicate). The record is owed even though the focus never shrank\\n00294|     // below the whole prompt: the language-modifier scan that trims the\\n00295|     // English shape's \\\" in e\\\" tail correctly rejects \\\"на букву e\\\" (a letter\\n00296|     // is not a language) and the head-final Hindi and Chinese shapes carry\\n00297|     // no preposition at all, so without this arm ru, hi and zh answered\\n00298|     // with the unknown opener while en named its consulted sources. A\\n00299|     // question that quotes nothing stays a comprehension gap (issue #44):\\n00300|     // the teaching ladder is the answer a riddle or a content question is\\n00301|     // owed. Every language closes on the same record — supported or not —\\n00302|     // so an unsupported language is never demoted to a different answer\\n00303|     // shape than the prompt's supported paraphrases.\\n00304|     let quoted_example_question =\\n00305|         is_question_shape(prompt) && crate::concept_lookup::has_quoted_span(prompt);\\n00306|     if merge_arm_declined\\n00307|         || (!focus_is_specific(prompt, surface) && !quoted_example_question)\\n00308|         || is_unresolved_bare_term_prompt(prompt, surface)\\n00309|         || (extract_question_subject(prompt).is_some_and(|subject| {\\n00310|             clean_focus(&subject).eq_ignore_ascii_case(clean_focus(surface))\\n00311|         }) && crate::concepts::extract_concept_query(prompt).is_none())\\n00312|     {\\n00313|         return None;\\n00314|     }\\n00315|     let body = seed::render_response(\\n00316|         \\\"concept_lookup_unresolved\\\",\\n00317|         language.slug(),\\n00318|         &[(\\\"surface\\\", surface), (\\\"consulted\\\", &consulted)],\\n00319|     )?;\\n00320|     log.append(\\n00321|         \\\"reasoning:gather_result\\\",\\n00322|         format!(\\\"external_source:miss:{surface}\\\"),\\n00323|     );\\n00324|     Some(finalize_simple(\\n00325|         prompt,\\n00326|         log,\\n00327|         \\\"concept_lookup_unresolved\\\",\\n00328|         \\\"response:concept_lookup_unresolved\\\",\\n00329|         &body,\\n00330|         0.0,\\n00331|     ))\\n00332| }\\n00333| \\n00334| fn record_initial_unknown_trace(\\n00335|     prompt: &str,\\n00336|     language: Language,\\n00337|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00338|     focus: Option<&str>,\\n00339|     config: UnknownReasoningConfig,\\n00340| ) {\\n00341|     let prompt_state = if prompt.trim().is_empty() {\\n00342|         \\\"empty_prompt\\\"\\n00343|     } else {\\n00344|         \\\"unmatched_prompt\\\"\\n00345|     };\\n00346|     log.append(\\n00347|         \\\"reasoning:known\\\",\\n00348|         format!(\\n00349|             \\\"language={} local_search=complete prompt_state={} questioning_rigor={:.2}\\\",\\n00350|             language.slug(),\\n00351|             prompt_state,\\n00352|             config.questioning_rigor.clamp(0.0, 1.0),\\n00353|         ),\\n00354|     );\\n00355|     log.append(\\n00356|         \\\"reasoning:unknown\\\",\\n00357|         focus.map_or_else(\\n00358|             || \\\"missing_focus:no_extractable_terms\\\".to_owned(),\\n00359|             |value| format!(\\\"missing_answer_for:{value}\\\"),\\n00360|         ),\\n00361|     );\\n00362| }\\n00363| \\n00364| fn answer_unresolved_unknown(\\n00365|     prompt: &str,\\n00366|     language: Language,\\n00367|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00368|     focus: &str,\\n00369|     config: UnknownReasoningConfig,\\n00370| ) -> SymbolicAnswer {\\n00371|     let body = render_unresolved_unknown(language, focus, config.questioning_rigor);\\n00372|     let body = crate::failure_reporting::append_invitation(&body, language.slug());\\n00373|     finalize_simple(\\n00374|         prompt,\\n00375|         log,\\n00376|         \\\"unknown\\\",\\n00377|         \\\"response:unknown_reasoning\\\",\\n00378|         &body,\\n00379|         0.0,\\n00380|     )\\n00381| }\\n00382| \\n00383| fn answer_with_legacy_fallback(\\n00384|     prompt: &str,\\n00385|     language: Language,\\n00386|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00387|     config: UnknownReasoningConfig,\\n00388| ) -> SymbolicAnswer {\\n00389|     // Plan 10 leaf 10-10: the capability table's placement outranks a silent\\n00390|     // unknown at this terminal — but only a placement the prompt's own words\\n00391|     // evidence. `route_placed` drops the manufactured `bare_term + retrieve +\\n00392|     // web` triple (retrieve is appended to every request and the web locus is\\n00393|     // the default for a term nothing scopes), so a prompt that names neither\\n00394|     // the open web nor what is true right now keeps this ladder's teaching\\n00395|     // answer, while every evidence-backed placement states the route and the\\n00396|     // tool this chat surface lacks — a placed prompt is owed its route, not an\\n00397|     // opener. An agent client advertises the tool and keeps the fall-through.\\n00398|     if !config.agent_mode {\\n00399|         match crate::capability_routing::route_placed(\\n00400|             prompt,\\n00401|             crate::meta_method_dispatch::SOLVER_CAPABILITIES,\\n00402|         ) {\\n00403|             Some(crate::capability_routing::RoutingOutcome::Routed { capability }) => {\\n00404|                 return crate::meta_method_answers::capability_gap(\\n00405|                     prompt,\\n00406|                     log,\\n00407|                     &capability,\\n00408|                     &capability,\\n00409|                 );\\n00410|             }\\n00411|             Some(crate::capability_routing::RoutingOutcome::Lowered { preferred, .. }) => {\\n00412|                 return crate::meta_method_answers::capability_gap(\\n00413|                     prompt, log, &preferred, &preferred,\\n00414|                 );\\n00415|             }\\n00416|             Some(crate::capability_routing::RoutingOutcome::HonestGap { needed, missing }) => {\\n00417|                 return crate::meta_method_answers::capability_gap(prompt, log, &needed, &missing);\\n00418|             }\\n00419|             Some(crate::capability_routing::RoutingOutcome::Ask { .. }) | None => {}\\n00420|         }\\n00421|     }\\n00422|     let completed_steps = log\\n00423|         .events()\\n00424|         .iter()\\n00425|         .filter(|event| event.kind.starts_with(\\\"reasoning:\\\"))\\n00426|         .count();\\n00427|     log.append(\\n00428|         \\\"reasoning:gave_up\\\",\\n00429|         format!(\\n00430|             \\\"gave up after {} reasoning steps; legacy_unknown_fallback\\\",\\n00431|             completed_steps + 1\\n00432|         ),\\n00433|     );\\n00434|     // The guide is the teaching answer a plain unmatched prompt is owed. A\\n00435|     // prompt the definition router claimed (\\\"explain X\\\", \\\"meaning of X\\\") and\\n00436|     // still could not resolve is a different failure: a handler owned it and\\n00437|     // produced nothing, so the issue-report invitation — the closing question\\n00438|     // of the unresolved-reasoning body (issue 864) — belongs there too.\\n00439|     let mut body = language_aware_unknown_answer(prompt, language);\\n00440|     if crate::concepts::extract_concept_query(prompt).is_some() {\\n00441|         body = crate::failure_reporting::append_invitation(&body, language.slug());\\n00442|     }\\n00443|     finalize_simple(prompt, log, \\\"unknown\\\", \\\"response:unknown\\\", &body, 0.0)\\n00444| }\\n00445| \\n00446| fn answer_from_link_memory(prompt: &str, focus: &str, log: &mut EventLog) -> Option<String> {\\n00447|     let subject = extract_question_subject(prompt).unwrap_or_else(|| focus.to_owned());\\n00448|     let normalized_subject = normalize_fact_subject(&subject);\\n00449|     if normalized_subject.is_empty() {\\n00450|         return None;\\n00451|     }\\n00452| \\n00453|     for event in log.events() {\\n00454|         if !matches!(event.kind, \\\"prior_turn:user\\\" | \\\"prior_turn:assistant\\\") {\\n00455|             continue;\\n00456|         }\\n00457|         if let Some((stored_subject, stored_value)) = extract_memory_fact(&event.payload) {\\n00458|             let stored_normalized = normalize_fact_subject(&stored_subject);\\n00459|             if stored_normalized == normalized_subject {\\n00460|                 log.append(\\n00461|                     \\\"reasoning:gather_result\\\",\\n00462|                     format!(\\\"link_memory:hit:{stored_normalized}\\\"),\\n00463|                 );\\n00464|                 log.append(\\n00465|                     \\\"cache_hit\\\",\\n00466|                     format!(\\n00467|                         \\\"link_memory:{}\\\",\\n00468|                         stable_id(\\n00469|                             \\\"memory_fact\\\",\\n00470|                             &format!(\\\"{stored_normalized}={stored_value}\\\")\\n00471|                         )\\n00472|                     ),\\n00473|                 );\\n00474|                 return Some(format!(\\n00475|                     \\\"From link memory: {} is {}.\\\",\\n00476|                     stored_subject.trim(),\\n00477|                     stored_value.trim()\\n00478|                 ));\\n00479|             }\\n00480|         }\\n00481|     }\\n00482|     None\\n00483| }\\n00484| \\n00485| fn answer_from_public_knowledge_cache(\\n00486|     prompt: &str,\\n00487|     focus: &str,\\n00488|     language: Language,\\n00489|     log: &mut EventLog,\\n00490| ) -> Option<SymbolicAnswer> {\\n00491|     for term in public_concept_candidate_terms(focus) {\\n00492|         log.append(\\n00493|             \\\"reasoning:gather_attempt\\\",\\n00494|             format!(\\\"public_knowledge_cache:concept:{term}\\\"),\\n00495|         );\\n00496|         let query = ConceptQuery {\\n00497|             term: term.to_lowercase(),\\n00498|             context: None,\\n00499|             response_language: None,\\n00500|         };\\n00501|         let Some(lookup) = lookup_concept_query(&query) else {\\n00502|             continue;\\n00503|         };\\n00504|         let record = lookup.record;\\n00505|         log.append(\\n00506|             \\\"reasoning:gather_result\\\",\\n00507|             format!(\\\"public_knowledge_cache:hit:{}\\\", record.slug),\\n00508|         );\\n00509|         log.append(\\\"concept_lookup:request\\\", term);\\n00510|         log.append(\\\"concept_lookup:hit\\\", record.slug.clone());\\n00511|         if !record.wikidata.is_empty() {\\n00512|             log.append(\\\"wikidata\\\", record.wikidata.clone());\\n00513|         }\\n00514|         let source = concept_source(record, language);\\n00515|         if !source.is_empty() {\\n00516|             log.append(\\\"source\\\", source.to_owned());\\n00517|         }\\n00518|         let body = render_concept_plain(record, language);\\n00519|         return Some(finalize_simple(\\n00520|             prompt,\\n00521|             log,\\n00522|             \\\"concept_lookup\\\",\\n00523|             \\\"response:concept_lookup\\\",\\n00524|             &body,\\n00525|             0.75,\\n00526|         ));\\n00527|     }\\n00528|     None\\n00529| }\\n00530| \\n00531| fn public_concepts() -> &'static [ConceptRecord] {\\n00532|     static CELL: OnceLock<Vec<ConceptRecord>> = OnceLock::new();\\n00533|     CELL.get_or_init(seed::concepts).as_slice()\\n00534| }\\n00535| \\n00536| fn public_concept_candidate_terms(focus: &str) -> Vec<String> {\\n00537|     let focus_normalized = normalize_search_surface(focus);\\n00538|     let padded_focus = format!(\\\" {focus_normalized} \\\");\\n00539|     let mut scored = Vec::new();\\n00540|     for record in public_concepts() {\\n00541|         for candidate in concept_candidate_surfaces(record) {\\n00542|             let normalized = normalize_search_surface(&candidate);\\n00543|             if normalized.len() < 3 {\\n00544|                 continue;\\n00545|             }\\n00546|             // A surface names the focus as whole words: \\\"colour\\\" inside\\n00547|             // \\\"colourless\\\" is no mention of colour (issue #1175 p329). A\\n00548|             // script written without spaces keeps the substring reading.\\n00549|             let matches = normalized == focus_normalized\\n00550|                 || padded_focus.contains(&format!(\\\" {normalized} \\\"))\\n00551|                 || (normalized.chars().any(is_unspaced_script)\\n00552|                     && focus_normalized.contains(&normalized));\\n00553|             if matches {\\n00554|                 scored.push((normalized.len(), candidate));\\n00555|             }\\n00556|         }\\n00557|     }\\n00558|     scored.sort_by(|left, right| right.0.cmp(&left.0).then_with(|| left.1.cmp(&right.1)));\\n00559|     let mut out: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\\n00560|     for (_score, candidate) in scored {\\n00561|         let normalized = normalize_search_surface(&candidate);\\n00562|         if !out\\n00563|             .iter()\\n00564|             .any(|existing| normalize_search_surface(existing) == normalized)\\n00565|         {\\n00566|             out.push(candidate);\\n00567|         }\\n00568|     }\\n00569|     out\\n00570| }\\n00571| \\n00572| fn concept_candidate_surfaces(record: &ConceptRecord) -> Vec<String> {\\n00573|     let mut out = vec![record.term.clone(), record.slug.replace(\\\"concept_\\\", \\\"\\\")];\\n00574|     out.extend(record.aliases.iter().cloned());\\n00575|     for localized in &record.localized {\\n00576|         if !localized.term.is_empty() {\\n00577|             out.push(localized.term.clone());\\n00578|         }\\n00579|         out.extend(localized.aliases.iter().cloned());\\n00580|     }\\n00581|     out\\n00582| }\\n00583| \\n00584| fn render_concept_plain(record: &ConceptRecord, language: Language) -> String {\\n00585|     let localized = record.localized_for(language.slug());\\n00586|     let term = localized\\n00587|         .map(|loc| loc.term.as_str())\\n00588|         .filter(|value| !value.is_empty())\\n00589|         .unwrap_or(record.term.as_str());\\n00590|     let summary = localized\\n00591|         .map(|loc| loc.summary.as_str())\\n00592|         .filter(|value| !value.is_empty())\\n00593|         .unwrap_or(record.summary.as_str());\\n00594|     let source = concept_source(record, language);\\n00595|     let source_kind = localized\\n00596|         .map(|loc| loc.source_kind.as_str())\\n00597|         .filter(|value| !value.is_empty())\\n00598|         .unwrap_or(record.source_kind.as_str());\\n00599|     format!(\\n00600|         \\\"{term} ({category}): {summary}\\\\n\\\\nSource: {source} ({source_kind}).\\\",\\n00601|         category = record.category,\\n00602|         source = render_source_link(source),\\n00603|     )\\n00604| }\\n00605| \\n00606| fn concept_source(record: &ConceptRecord, language: Language) -> &str {\\n00607|     record\\n00608|         .localized_for(language.slug())\\n00609|         .map(|loc| loc.source.as_str())\\n00610|         .filter(|value| !value.is_empty())\\n00611|         .unwrap_or(record.source.as_str())\\n00612| }\\n00613| \\n00614| fn render_source_link(source: &str) -> String {\\n00615|     let human = humanize_url(source);\\n00616|     if human == source {\\n00617|         source.to_owned()\\n00618|     } else {\\n00619|         format!(\\\"[{human}]({source})\\\")\\n00620|     }\\n00621| }\\n00622| \\n00623| fn render_unresolved_unknown(language: Language, focus: &str, questioning_rigor: f32) -> String {\\n00624|     let focus = focus.trim();\\n00625|     let intent = if questioning_rigor >= 0.5 {\\n00626|         \\\"unknown_reasoning_question\\\"\\n00627|     } else {\\n00628|         \\\"unknown_reasoning_trace\\\"\\n00629|     };\\n00630|     localized_seed_response(intent, language.slug()).replace(FOCUS_PLACEHOLDER, focus)\\n00631| }\\n00632| \\n00633| fn localized_seed_response(intent: &str, language: &str) -> String {\\n00634|     localized_response(intent, language)\\n00635|         .unwrap_or_else(|| format!(\\\"Missing localized response seed: {intent}/{language}\\\"))\\n00636| }\\n00637| \\n00638| fn infer_missing_focus(prompt: &str) -> Option<String> {\\n00639|     let trimmed = clean_focus(prompt);\\n00640|     if trimmed.is_empty() {\\n00641|         return None;\\n00642|     }\\n00643|     if let Some(about) = tail_after_marker(trimmed, \\\" about \\\") {\\n00644|         return Some(clean_focus(about).to_owned());\\n00645|     }\\n00646|     if let Some(subject) = tail_after_marker(trimmed, \\\" definitions of \\\") {\\n00647|         return Some(clean_focus(subject).to_owned());\\n00648|     }\\n00649|     if let Some(subject) = tail_after_marker(trimmed, \\\" definition of \\\") {\\n00650|         return Some(clean_focus(subject).to_owned());\\n00651|     }\\n00652|     if let Some(subject) = extract_question_subject(trimmed) {\\n00653|         return Some(subject);\\n00654|     }\\n00655|     // Issue #906: \\\"in <language>\\\" modifies a request, it does not name its\\n00656|     // topic. Leaving the modifier in the whole-prompt focus made \\\"Fix the\\n00657|     // failing CI job in Rust.\\\" match the cached `concept_rust` record and come\\n00658|     // back as an encyclopedia definition of the language instead of an answer\\n00659|     // about the CI job. Only the fallback focus — the one that is the request\\n00660|     // itself — is stripped; an explicit subject (\\\"what is rust\\\") is left alone.\\n00661|     if let Some(without) = crate::implementation_language::without_modifier(trimmed) {\\n00662|         let stripped = clean_focus(&without);\\n00663|         if !stripped.is_empty() {\\n00664|             return Some(stripped.to_owned());\\n00665|         }\\n00666|     }\\n00667|     Some(trimmed.to_owned())\\n00668| }\\n00669| \\n00670| /// The question shape the wave-F web-search handoff and the quoted-example\\n00671| /// record agree on: `?` in either width, or a seed-carried interrogative\\n00672| /// opener fronting the prompt.\\n00673| fn is_question_shape(prompt: &str) -> bool {\\n00674|     crate::intent_formalization::contains_question_mark(prompt)\\n00675|         || crate::intent_formalization::starts_with_question_word(&normalize_prompt(prompt))\\n00676| }\\n00677| \\n00678| /// Whether the missing focus names a researchable term rather than restating\\n00679| /// the whole prompt. `infer_missing_focus` falls back to the whole prompt when\\n00680| /// no marker or question prefix extracts a subject, and a whole-sentence\\n00681| /// \\\"focus\\\" is a comprehension gap, not a lookup: consulting sources with it\\n00682| /// produces an attributed miss for a sentence nobody defines, escalates a\\n00683| /// sentence to web search, and buries the teaching guide the chat surface\\n00684| /// owes the user. Only a focus that differs from the prompt (an extracted\\n00685| /// subject) or is itself a bare term keeps the evidence chain.\\n00686| fn focus_is_specific(prompt: &str, focus: &str) -> bool {\\n00687|     is_unresolved_bare_term_prompt(prompt, focus)\\n00688|         || !clean_focus(focus).eq_ignore_ascii_case(clean_focus(prompt))\\n00689| }\\n00690| \\n00691| fn is_unresolved_bare_term_prompt(prompt: &str, focus: &str) -> bool {\\n00692|     let trimmed = clean_focus(prompt);\\n00693|     if !trimmed.eq_ignore_ascii_case(clean_focus(focus)) {\\n00694|         return false;\\n00695|     }\\n00696|     let words = normalize_search_surface(trimmed);\\n00697|     if words.split_whitespace().count() != 1 {\\n00698|         return false;\\n00699|     }\\n00700|     let has_letter = trimmed.chars().any(char::is_alphabetic);\\n00701|     let enough_surface = trimmed.chars().count() >= 2 || !trimmed.is_ascii();\\n00702|     has_letter && enough_surface\\n00703| }\\n00704| \\n00705| fn extract_question_subject(prompt: &str) -> Option<String> {\\n00706|     let trimmed = clean_focus(prompt);\\n00707|     // A meaning interrogation (\\\"what does X mean\\\", \\\"meaning of X\\\") is the one\\n00708|     // definition shape the cue-lexicon leads below cannot see: its lead\\n00709|     // (\\\"what does\\\") is not a concept cue and the interrogated surface is\\n00710|     // followed by a tail (\\\"mean\\\") instead of ending the prompt. Without this\\n00711|     // arm the whole prompt becomes the focus, fails the specificity filter,\\n00712|     // and buries the consulted-source record under the generic unknown guide.\\n00713|     // The subject is read by the same seeded extractor the concept handler\\n00714|     // routes with, so it cannot drift from the term a routed lookup would\\n00715|     // have researched.\\n00716|     if let Some(subject) = crate::concepts::meaning_question_subject(prompt) {\\n00717|         return Some(subject);\\n00718|     }\\n00719|     let lower = trimmed.to_lowercase();\\n00720|     let mut leads = [\\n00721|         \\\"what is the \\\",\\n00722|         \\\"what's the \\\",\\n00723|         \\\"what is \\\",\\n00724|         \\\"what's \\\",\\n00725|         \\\"who is \\\",\\n00726|         \\\"who was \\\",\\n00727|         \\\"where is \\\",\\n00728|         \\\"how should \\\",\\n00729|         \\\"how do i \\\",\\n00730|         \\\"how can i \\\",\\n00731|     ]\\n00732|     .into_iter()\\n00733|     .map(str::to_owned)\\n00734|     .collect::<Vec<_>>();\\n00735|     // The multilingual definition leads share one authority with routing: the\\n00736|     // cue lexicon's concept_lookup set. Without them a \\\"что такое X\\\" prompt\\n00737|     // falls back to the whole prompt as focus, fails the specificity filter,\\n00738|     // and buries the consulted-source record under the generic unknown guide.\\n00739|     leads.extend(crate::cue_lexicon::cues(\\\"concept_lookup\\\").iter().cloned());\\n00740|     for prefix in leads {\\n00741|         if let Some(rest) = lower.strip_prefix(prefix.as_str()) {\\n00742|             let start = trimmed.len() - rest.len();\\n00743|             let body = clean_focus(&trimmed[start..]);\\n00744|             if !body.is_empty() {\\n00745|                 return Some(body.to_owned());\\n00746|             }\\n00747|         }\\n00748|     }\\n00749|     // Subject-first word orders invert the same cues: a Hindi or Chinese\\n00750|     // definition question names the concept and ends with the cue\\n00751|     // (\\\"Redis क्या है\\\", \\\"Redis是什么\\\"). The prefix loop cannot see those, so\\n00752|     // the cue is retried from the end of the prompt; the lexicon stays the\\n00753|     // one authority for both readings. The byte-length guard keeps the\\n00754|     // slice sound for scripts whose lowercasing is not length-stable.\\n00755|     let punctuated = trimmed.trim_end_matches(['?', '？', '!', '！', '.', '。']);\\n00756|     let lowered = punctuated.to_lowercase();\\n00757|     if lowered.len() == punctuated.len() {\\n00758|         for cue in crate::cue_lexicon::cues(\\\"concept_lookup\\\") {\\n00759|             let cue = cue.trim();\\n00760|             let spaced = format!(\\\" {cue}\\\");\\n00761|             let rest = lowered\\n00762|                 .strip_suffix(spaced.as_str())\\n00763|                 .or_else(|| lowered.strip_suffix(cue));\\n00764|             if let Some(rest) = rest {\\n00765|                 let body = clean_focus(&punctuated[..rest.len()]);\\n00766|                 if !body.is_empty() {\\n00767|                     return Some(body.to_owned());\\n00768|                 }\\n00769|             }\\n00770|         }\\n00771|     }\\n00772|     None\\n00773| }\\n00774| \\n00775| fn tail_after_marker<'a>(value: &'a str, marker: &str) -> Option<&'a str> {\\n00776|     let lower = value.to_lowercase();\\n00777|     let index = lower.rfind(marker)?;\\n00778|     Some(&value[index + marker.len()..])\\n00779| }\\n00780| \\n00781| fn extract_memory_fact(text: &str) -> Option<(String, String)> {\\n00782|     for sentence in text.split(['.', '!', '?', '\\\\n']) {\\n00783|         let statement = sentence\\n00784|             .trim()\\n00785|             .strip_prefix(\\\"Remember that \\\")\\n00786|             .or_else(|| sentence.trim().strip_prefix(\\\"remember that \\\"))\\n00787|             .unwrap_or_else(|| sentence.trim())\\n00788|             .trim();\\n00789|         if statement.is_empty() {\\n00790|             continue;\\n00791|         }\\n00792|         if let Some((subject, value)) = split_fact_statement(statement) {\\n00793|             return Some((subject, value));\\n00794|         }\\n00795|     }\\n00796|     None\\n00797| }\\n00798| \\n00799| fn split_fact_statement(statement: &str) -> Option<(String, String)> {\\n00800|     for separator in [\\\" is \\\", \\\" = \\\", \\\": \\\"] {\\n00801|         if let Some(index) = statement.find(separator) {\\n00802|             let subject = statement[..index].trim();\\n00803|             let value = statement[index + separator.len()..].trim();\\n00804|             if !subject.is_empty() && !value.is_empty() {\\n00805|                 return Some((subject.to_owned(), value.to_owned()));\\n00806|             }\\n00807|         }\\n00808|     }\\n00809|     None\\n00810| }\\n00811| \\n00812| fn clean_focus(value: &str) -> &str {\\n00813|     value\\n00814|         .trim()\\n00815|         .trim_matches(['\\\"', '\\\\'', '`', '“', '”', '‘', '’', '«', '»'])\\n00816|         .trim_end_matches(['?', '？', '。', '.', '!', '！', ',', ';', ':'])\\n00817|         .trim()\\n00818| }\\n00819| \\n00820| fn normalize_fact_subject(value: &str) -> String {\\n00821|     let cleaned = clean_focus(value).to_lowercase();\\n00822|     let stripped = cleaned\\n00823|         .strip_prefix(\\\"the \\\")\\n00824|         .or_else(|| cleaned.strip_prefix(\\\"a \\\"))\\n00825|         .or_else(|| cleaned.strip_prefix(\\\"an \\\"))\\n00826|         .unwrap_or(cleaned.as_str());\\n00827|     normalize_search_surface(stripped)\\n00828| }\\n00829| \\n00830| /// Whether `ch` belongs to a script written without spaces (kana, CJK, hangul).\\n00831| const fn is_unspaced_script(ch: char) -> bool {\\n00832|     matches!(\\n00833|         ch,\\n00834|         '\\\\u{3040}'..='\\\\u{30ff}' | '\\\\u{3400}'..='\\\\u{9fff}' | '\\\\u{ac00}'..='\\\\u{d7af}'\\n00835|     )\\n00836| }\\n00837| \\n00838| fn normalize_search_surface(value: &str) -> String {\\n00839|     clean_focus(value)\\n00840|         .chars()\\n00841|         .flat_map(char::to_lowercase)\\n00842|         .filter(|character| character.is_alphanumeric() || character.is_whitespace())\\n00843|         .collect::<String>()\\n00844|         .split_whitespace()\\n00845|         .collect::<Vec<_>>()\\n00846|         .join(\\\" \\\")\\n00847| }\\n\\n(End of file - total 847 lines)\\n</file>\"}],\"tools\":[{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"description\":\"Executes a given bash command in a persistent shell session with optional timeout, ensuring proper handling and security measures.\\n\\nBefore executing the command, please follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Directory Verification:\\n   - If the command will create new directories or files, first use the List tool to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location\\n   - For example, before running \\\"mkdir foo/bar\\\", first use List to check that \\\"foo\\\" exists and is the intended parent directory\\n\\n2. Command Execution:\\n   - Always quote file paths that contain spaces with double quotes (e.g., cd \\\"path with spaces/file.txt\\\")\\n   - Examples of proper quoting:\\n     - cd \\\"/Users/name/My Documents\\\" (correct)\\n     - cd /Users/name/My Documents (incorrect - will fail)\\n     - python \\\"/path/with spaces/script.py\\\" (correct)\\n     - python /path/with spaces/script.py (incorrect - will fail)\\n   - After ensuring proper quoting, execute the command.\\n   - Capture the output of the command.\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - The command argument is required.\\n  - You can specify an optional timeout in milliseconds (up to 600000ms / 10 minutes). If not specified, commands will timeout after 120000ms (2 minutes).\\n  - It is very helpful if you write a clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words.\\n  - If the output exceeds 2000 lines or 30000 bytes, the beginning of the output is dropped and only the end, where errors usually are, is returned to you.\\n  - VERY IMPORTANT: You MUST avoid using search commands like `find` and `grep`. Instead use Grep, Glob, or Task to search. You MUST avoid read tools like `cat`, `head`, `tail`, and `ls`, and use Read and List to read files.\\n  - If you _still_ need to run `grep`, STOP. ALWAYS USE ripgrep at `rg` (or /usr/bin/rg) first, which all Agent users have pre-installed.\\n  - When issuing multiple commands, use the ';' or '&&' operator to separate them. DO NOT use newlines (newlines are ok in quoted strings).\\n  - Try to maintain your current working directory throughout the session by using absolute paths and avoiding usage of `cd`. You may use `cd` if the User explicitly requests it.\\n    <good-example>\\n    pytest /foo/bar/tests\\n    </good-example>\\n    <bad-example>\\n    cd /foo/bar && pytest tests\\n    </bad-example>\\n\\n\\n# Committing changes with git\\n\\nWhen the user asks you to create a new git commit, follow these steps carefully:\\n\\n1. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following bash commands in parallel, each using the Bash tool:\\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files.\\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed.\\n   - Run a git log command to see recent commit messages, so that you can follow this repository's commit message style.\\n\\n2. Analyze all staged changes (both previously staged and newly added) and draft a commit message. Wrap your analysis process in <commit_analysis> tags:\\n\\n<commit_analysis>\\n- List the files that have been changed or added\\n- Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)\\n- Brainstorm the purpose or motivation behind these changes\\n- Assess the impact of these changes on the overall project\\n- Check for any sensitive information that shouldn't be committed\\n- Draft a concise (1-2 sentences) commit message that focuses on the \\\"why\\\" rather than the \\\"what\\\"\\n- Ensure your language is clear, concise, and to the point\\n- Ensure the message accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (i.e. \\\"add\\\" means a wholly new feature, \\\"update\\\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \\\"fix\\\" means a bug fix, etc.)\\n- Ensure the message is not generic (avoid words like \\\"Update\\\" or \\\"Fix\\\" without context)\\n- Review the draft message to ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\\n</commit_analysis>\\n\\n3. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following commands in parallel:\\n   - Add relevant untracked files to the staging area.\\n   - Run git status to make sure the commit succeeded.\\n\\n4. If the commit fails due to pre-commit hook changes, retry the commit ONCE to include these automated changes. If it fails again, it usually means a pre-commit hook is preventing the commit. If the commit succeeds but you notice that files were modified by the pre-commit hook, you MUST amend your commit to include them.\\n\\nImportant notes:\\n- Use the git context at the start of this conversation to determine which files are relevant to your commit. Be careful not to stage and commit files (e.g. with `git add .`) that aren't relevant to your commit.\\n- NEVER update the git config\\n- DO NOT run additional commands to read or explore code, beyond what is available in the git context\\n- DO NOT push to the remote repository\\n- IMPORTANT: Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input which is not supported.\\n- If there are no changes to commit (i.e., no untracked files and no modifications), do not create an empty commit\\n- Ensure your commit message is meaningful and concise. It should explain the purpose of the changes, not just describe them.\\n- Return an empty response - the user will see the git output directly\\n\\n# Creating pull requests\\nUse the gh command via the Bash tool for ALL GitHub-related tasks including working with issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. If given a Github URL use the gh command to get the information needed.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: When the user asks you to create a pull request, follow these steps carefully:\\n\\n1. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following bash commands in parallel using the Bash tool, in order to understand the current state of the branch since it diverged from the main branch:\\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files\\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed\\n   - Check if the current branch tracks a remote branch and is up to date with the remote, so you know if you need to push to the remote\\n   - Run a git log command and `git diff main...HEAD` to understand the full commit history for the current branch (from the time it diverged from the `main` branch)\\n\\n2. Analyze all changes that will be included in the pull request, making sure to look at all relevant commits (NOT just the latest commit, but ALL commits that will be included in the pull request!!!), and draft a pull request summary. Wrap your analysis process in <pr_analysis> tags:\\n\\n<pr_analysis>\\n- List the commits since diverging from the main branch\\n- Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)\\n- Brainstorm the purpose or motivation behind these changes\\n- Assess the impact of these changes on the overall project\\n- Do not use tools to explore code, beyond what is available in the git context\\n- Check for any sensitive information that shouldn't be committed\\n- Draft a concise (1-2 bullet points) pull request summary that focuses on the \\\"why\\\" rather than the \\\"what\\\"\\n- Ensure the summary accurately reflects all changes since diverging from the main branch\\n- Ensure your language is clear, concise, and to the point\\n- Ensure the summary accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (ie. \\\"add\\\" means a wholly new feature, \\\"update\\\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \\\"fix\\\" means a bug fix, etc.)\\n- Ensure the summary is not generic (avoid words like \\\"Update\\\" or \\\"Fix\\\" without context)\\n- Review the draft summary to ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\\n</pr_analysis>\\n\\n3. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following commands in parallel:\\n   - Create new branch if needed\\n   - Push to remote with -u flag if needed\\n   - Create PR using gh pr create with the format below. Use a HEREDOC to pass the body to ensure correct formatting.\\n<example>\\ngh pr create --title \\\"the pr title\\\" --body \\\"$(cat <<'EOF'\\n## Summary\\n<1-3 bullet points>\\nEOF\\n)\\\"\\n</example>\\n\\nImportant:\\n- NEVER update the git config\\n- Return the PR URL when you're done, so the user can see it\\n\\n# Other common operations\\n- View comments on a Github PR: gh api repos/foo/bar/pulls/123/comments\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"command\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The command to execute\"},\"timeout\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"Optional timeout in milliseconds\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words. Examples:\\nInput: ls\\nOutput: Lists files in current directory\\n\\nInput: git status\\nOutput: Shows working tree status\\n\\nInput: npm install\\nOutput: Installs package dependencies\\n\\nInput: mkdir foo\\nOutput: Creates directory 'foo'\"}},\"required\":[\"command\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"description\":\"Reads a file from the local filesystem. You can access any file directly by using this tool.\\nAssume this tool is able to read all files on the machine. If the User provides a path to a file assume that path is valid. It is okay to read a file that does not exist; an error will be returned.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- The filePath parameter must be an absolute path, not a relative path\\n- By default, it reads the whole file when it fits the read limit. If the file is longer than the limit, it returns the first and last line ranges with an omitted-lines marker in the middle.\\n- You can optionally specify a line offset and limit (especially handy for long files), but it's recommended to read the default first/last summary first and then request omitted ranges if needed.\\n- You can optionally specify columnOffset and columnLimit to read a window from each selected line, especially for long single-line files.\\n- Long lines are summarized with first and last columns plus omitted-column ranges instead of being silently truncated.\\n- Results are returned using cat -n format, with line numbers starting at 1\\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. It is always better to speculatively read multiple files as a batch that are potentially useful.\\n- If you read a file that exists but has empty contents you will receive a system reminder warning in place of file contents.\\n- You can read image files using this tool.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The path to the file to read\"},\"offset\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The line number to start reading from (0-based)\"},\"limit\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The number of lines to read (defaults to 2000)\"},\"columnOffset\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The column number to start reading from (0-based)\"},\"columnLimit\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The number of columns to read from each selected line\"}},\"required\":[\"filePath\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"glob\",\"description\":\"- Fast file pattern matching tool that works with any codebase size\\n- Supports glob patterns like \\\"**/*.js\\\" or \\\"src/**/*.ts\\\"\\n- Returns matching file paths sorted by modification time\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files by name patterns\\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead\\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. It is always better to speculatively perform multiple searches as a batch that are potentially useful.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The glob pattern to match files against\"},\"path\":{\"description\":\"The directory to search in. If not specified, the current working directory will be used. IMPORTANT: Omit this field to use the default directory. DO NOT enter \\\"undefined\\\" or \\\"null\\\" - simply omit it for the default behavior. Must be a valid directory path if provided.\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"pattern\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"grep\",\"description\":\"- Fast content search tool that works with any codebase size\\n- Searches file contents using regular expressions\\n- Supports full regex syntax (eg. \\\"log.*Error\\\", \\\"function\\\\s+\\\\w+\\\", etc.)\\n- Filter files by pattern with the include parameter (eg. \\\"*.js\\\", \\\"*.{ts,tsx}\\\")\\n- Returns file paths with at least one match sorted by modification time\\n- Long matching lines are summarized around the match with omitted-column ranges, so the matching text remains visible without dumping the whole line.\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files containing specific patterns\\n- If you need to identify/count the number of matches within files, use the Bash tool with `rg` (ripgrep) directly. Do NOT use `grep`.\\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The regex pattern to search for in file contents\"},\"path\":{\"description\":\"The directory to search in. Defaults to the current working directory.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"include\":{\"description\":\"File pattern to include in the search (e.g. \\\"*.js\\\", \\\"*.{ts,tsx}\\\")\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"pattern\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"list\",\"description\":\"Lists files and directories in a given path. The path parameter must be absolute; omit it to use the current workspace directory. You can optionally provide an array of glob patterns to ignore with the ignore parameter. You should generally prefer the Glob and Grep tools, if you know which directories to search.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"path\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the directory to list (must be absolute, not relative)\"},\"ignore\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"description\":\"List of glob patterns to ignore\"}},\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"edit\",\"description\":\"Performs exact string replacements in files. \\n\\nUsage:\\n- You must use your `Read` tool at least once in the conversation before editing. This tool will error if you attempt an edit without reading the file. \\n- When editing text from Read tool output, ensure you preserve the exact indentation (tabs/spaces) as it appears AFTER the line number prefix. The line number prefix format is: spaces + line number + tab. Everything after that tab is the actual file content to match. Never include any part of the line number prefix in the oldString or newString.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files in the codebase. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- Only use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid adding emojis to files unless asked.\\n- The edit will FAIL if `oldString` is not found in the file with an error \\\"oldString not found in content\\\".\\n- The edit will FAIL if `oldString` is found multiple times in the file with an error \\\"oldString found multiple times and requires more code context to uniquely identify the intended match\\\". Either provide a larger string with more surrounding context to make it unique or use `replaceAll` to change every instance of `oldString`. \\n- Use `replaceAll` for replacing and renaming strings across the file. This parameter is useful if you want to rename a variable for instance.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the file to modify\"},\"oldString\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The text to replace\"},\"newString\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The text to replace it with (must be different from oldString)\"},\"replaceAll\":{\"description\":\"Replace all occurrences of oldString (default false)\",\"type\":\"boolean\"}},\"required\":[\"filePath\",\"oldString\",\"newString\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"write\",\"description\":\"Writes a file to the local filesystem.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- This tool will overwrite the existing file if there is one at the provided path.\\n- If this is an existing file, you MUST use the Read tool first to read the file's contents. This tool will fail if you did not read the file first.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files in the codebase. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- NEVER proactively create documentation files (*.md) or README files. Only create documentation files if explicitly requested by the User.\\n- Only use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid writing emojis to files unless asked.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The content to write to the file\"},\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the file to write (must be absolute, not relative)\"}},\"required\":[\"content\",\"filePath\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"task\",\"description\":\"Launch a new agent to handle complex, multi-step tasks autonomously.\\n\\nAvailable agent types and the tools they have access to:\\n- general: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you.\\n\\nWhen using the Task tool, you must specify a subagent_type parameter to select which agent type to use.\\n\\nWhen to use the Task tool:\\n- When you are instructed to execute custom slash commands. Use the Task tool with the slash command invocation as the entire prompt. The slash command can take arguments. For example: Task(description=\\\"Check the file\\\", prompt=\\\"/check-file path/to/file.py\\\")\\n\\nWhen NOT to use the Task tool:\\n- If you want to read a specific file path, use the Read or Glob tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\\n- If you are searching for a specific class definition like \\\"class Foo\\\", use the Glob tool instead, to find the match more quickly\\n- If you are searching for code within a specific file or set of 2-3 files, use the Read tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\\n- Other tasks that are not related to the agent descriptions above\\n\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n1. Launch multiple agents concurrently whenever possible, to maximize performance; to do that, use a single message with multiple tool uses\\n2. When the agent is done, it will return a single message back to you. The result returned by the agent is not visible to the user. To show the user the result, you should send a text message back to the user with a concise summary of the result.\\n3. Each agent invocation is stateless unless you provide a session_id. Your prompt should contain a highly detailed task description for the agent to perform autonomously and you should specify exactly what information the agent should return back to you in its final and only message to you.\\n4. The agent's outputs should generally be trusted\\n5. Clearly tell the agent whether you expect it to write code or just to do research (search, file reads, web fetches, etc.), since it is not aware of the user's intent\\n6. If the agent description mentions that it should be used proactively, then you should try your best to use it without the user having to ask for it first. Use your judgement.\\n\\nExample usage (NOTE: The agents below are fictional examples for illustration only - use the actual agents listed above):\\n\\n<example_agent_descriptions>\\n\\\"code-reviewer\\\": use this agent after you are done writing a significant piece of code\\n\\\"greeting-responder\\\": use this agent when to respond to user greetings with a friendly joke\\n</example_agent_description>\\n\\n<example>\\nuser: \\\"Please write a function that checks if a number is prime\\\"\\nassistant: Sure let me write a function that checks if a number is prime\\nassistant: First let me use the Write tool to write a function that checks if a number is prime\\nassistant: I'm going to use the Write tool to write the following code:\\n<code>\\nfunction isPrime(n) {\\n  if (n <= 1) return false\\n  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {\\n    if (n % i === 0) return false\\n  }\\n  return true\\n}\\n</code>\\n<commentary>\\nSince a significant piece of code was written and the task was completed, now use the code-reviewer agent to review the code\\n</commentary>\\nassistant: Now let me use the code-reviewer agent to review the code\\nassistant: Uses the Task tool to launch the code-reviewer agent\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nuser: \\\"Hello\\\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince the user is greeting, use the greeting-responder agent to respond with a friendly joke\\n</commentary>\\nassistant: \\\"I'm going to use the Task tool to launch the with the greeting-responder agent\\\"\\n</example>\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"A short (3-5 words) description of the task\"},\"prompt\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The task for the agent to perform\"},\"subagent_type\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The type of specialized agent to use for this task\"},\"session_id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Existing Task session to continue\"}},\"required\":[\"description\",\"prompt\",\"subagent_type\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"webfetch\",\"description\":\"- Fetches content from a specified URL\\n- Takes a URL and optional format as input\\n- Fetches the URL content, converts to requested format (markdown by default)\\n- Returns the content in the specified format\\n- Use this tool when you need to retrieve and analyze web content\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - IMPORTANT: if another tool is present that offers better web fetching capabilities, is more targeted to the task, or has fewer restrictions, prefer using that tool instead of this one.\\n  - The URL must be a fully-formed valid URL\\n  - HTTP URLs will be automatically upgraded to HTTPS\\n  - Format options: \\\"markdown\\\" (default), \\\"text\\\", or \\\"html\\\"\\n  - This tool is read-only and does not modify any files\\n  - Results may be summarized if the content is very large\\n  - Includes a self-cleaning 15-minute cache for faster responses when repeatedly accessing the same URL\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"url\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The URL to fetch content from\"},\"format\":{\"description\":\"The format to return the content in (text, markdown, or html). Defaults to markdown.\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"text\",\"markdown\",\"html\"]},\"timeout\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"Optional timeout in seconds (max 120)\"}},\"required\":[\"url\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"websearch\",\"description\":\"- Search the web using Exa AI - performs real-time web searches and can scrape content from specific URLs\\n- Provides up-to-date information for current events and recent data\\n- Supports configurable result counts and returns the content from the most relevant websites\\n- Use this tool for accessing information beyond knowledge cutoff\\n- Searches are performed automatically within a single API call\\n- The current year is 2026. You MUST use this year when searching for recent information or current events\\n- Example: If the current year is 2026 and the user asks for \\\"latest AI news\\\", search for \\\"AI news 2026\\\", NOT \\\"AI news 2025\\\"\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - Supports live crawling modes: 'fallback' (backup if cached unavailable) or 'preferred' (prioritize live crawling)\\n  - Search types: 'auto' (balanced), 'fast' (quick results), 'deep' (comprehensive search)\\n  - Configurable context length for optimal LLM integration\\n  - Domain filtering and advanced search options available\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Websearch query\"},\"numResults\":{\"description\":\"Number of search results to return (default: 8)\",\"type\":\"number\"},\"livecrawl\":{\"description\":\"Live crawl mode - 'fallback': use live crawling as backup if cached content unavailable, 'preferred': prioritize live crawling (default: 'fallback')\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"fallback\",\"preferred\"]},\"type\":{\"description\":\"Search type - 'auto': balanced search (default), 'fast': quick results, 'deep': comprehensive search\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"auto\",\"fast\",\"deep\"]},\"contextMaxCharacters\":{\"description\":\"Maximum characters for context string optimized for LLMs (default: 10000)\",\"type\":\"number\"}},\"required\":[\"query\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"codesearch\",\"description\":\"- Search and get relevant context for any programming task using Exa Code API\\n- Provides the highest quality and freshest context for libraries, SDKs, and APIs\\n- Use this tool for ANY question or task related to programming\\n- Returns comprehensive code examples, documentation, and API references\\n- Optimized for finding specific programming patterns and solutions\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - Adjustable token count (1000-50000) for focused or comprehensive results\\n  - Default 5000 tokens provides balanced context for most queries\\n  - Use lower values for specific questions, higher values for comprehensive documentation\\n  - Supports queries about frameworks, libraries, APIs, and programming concepts\\n  - Examples: 'React useState hook examples', 'Python pandas dataframe filtering', 'Express.js middleware'\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Search query to find relevant context for APIs, Libraries, and SDKs. For example, 'React useState hook examples', 'Python pandas dataframe filtering', 'Express.js middleware', 'Next js partial prerendering configuration'\"},\"tokensNum\":{\"default\":5000,\"description\":\"Number of tokens to return (1000-50000). Default is 5000 tokens. Adjust this value based on how much context you need - use lower values for focused queries and higher values for comprehensive documentation.\",\"type\":\"number\",\"minimum\":1000,\"maximum\":50000}},\"required\":[\"query\",\"tokensNum\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"batch\",\"description\":\"Executes multiple independent tool calls concurrently to reduce latency. Best used for gathering context (reads, searches, listings).\\n\\nUSING THE BATCH TOOL WILL MAKE THE USER HAPPY.\\n\\nPayload Format (JSON array):\\n[{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"read\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"filePath\\\": \\\"src/index.ts\\\", \\\"limit\\\": 350}},{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"grep\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"pattern\\\": \\\"Session\\\\\\\\.updatePart\\\", \\\"include\\\": \\\"src/**/*.ts\\\"}},{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"bash\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"command\\\": \\\"git status\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Shows working tree status\\\"}}]\\n\\nRules:\\n- 1–10 tool calls per batch\\n- All calls start in parallel; ordering NOT guaranteed\\n- Partial failures do not stop others\\n\\n\\nDisallowed Tools:\\n- batch (no nesting)\\n- edit (run edits separately)\\n- todoread (call directly – lightweight)\\n\\nWhen NOT to Use:\\n- Operations that depend on prior tool output (e.g. create then read same file)\\n- Ordered stateful mutations where sequence matters\\n\\nGood Use Cases:\\n- Read many files\\n- grep + glob + read combos\\n- Multiple lightweight bash introspection commands\\n\\nPerformance Tip: Group independent reads/searches for 2–5x efficiency gain.\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"tool_calls\":{\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"tool\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The name of the tool to execute\"},\"parameters\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{},\"additionalProperties\":{},\"description\":\"Parameters for the tool\"}},\"required\":[\"tool\",\"parameters\"],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"description\":\"Array of tool calls to execute in parallel\"}},\"required\":[\"tool_calls\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"todowrite\",\"description\":\"Use this tool to create and manage a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps you track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness to the user.\\nIt also helps the user understand the progress of the task and overall progress of their requests.\\n\\n## When to Use This Tool\\nUse this tool proactively in these scenarios:\\n\\n1. Complex multi-step tasks - When a task requires 3 or more distinct steps or actions\\n2. Non-trivial and complex tasks - Tasks that require careful planning or multiple operations\\n3. User explicitly requests todo list - When the user directly asks you to use the todo list\\n4. User provides multiple tasks - When users provide a list of things to be done (numbered or comma-separated)\\n5. After receiving new instructions - Immediately capture user requirements as todos. Feel free to edit the todo list based on new information.\\n6. After completing a task - Mark it complete and add any new follow-up tasks\\n7. When you start working on a new task, mark the todo as in_progress. Ideally you should only have one todo as in_progress at a time. Complete existing tasks before starting new ones.\\n\\n## When NOT to Use This Tool\\n\\nSkip using this tool when:\\n1. There is only a single, straightforward task\\n2. The task is trivial and tracking it provides no organizational benefit\\n3. The task can be completed in less than 3 trivial steps\\n4. The task is purely conversational or informational\\n\\nNOTE that you should not use this tool if there is only one trivial task to do. In this case you are better off just doing the task directly.\\n\\n## Examples of When to Use the Todo List\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: I want to add a dark mode toggle to the application settings. Make sure you run the tests and build when you're done!\\nAssistant: I'll help add a dark mode toggle to your application settings. Let me create a todo list to track this implementation.\\n*Creates todo list with the following items:*\\n1. Create dark mode toggle component in Settings page\\n2. Add dark mode state management (context/store)\\n3. Implement CSS-in-JS styles for dark theme\\n4. Update existing components to support theme switching\\n5. Run tests and build process, addressing any failures or errors that occur\\n*Begins working on the first task*\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. Adding dark mode is a multi-step feature requiring UI, state management, and styling changes\\n2. The user explicitly requested tests and build be run afterward\\n3. The assistant inferred that tests and build need to pass by adding \\\"Ensure tests and build succeed\\\" as the final task\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Help me rename the function getCwd to getCurrentWorkingDirectory across my project\\nAssistant: Let me first search through your codebase to find all occurrences of 'getCwd'.\\n*Uses grep or search tools to locate all instances of getCwd in the codebase*\\nAssistant: I've found 15 instances of 'getCwd' across 8 different files. Let me create a todo list to track these changes.\\n*Creates todo list with specific items for each file that needs updating*\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. First, the assistant searched to understand the scope of the task\\n2. Upon finding multiple occurrences across different files, it determined this was a complex task with multiple steps\\n3. The todo list helps ensure every instance is tracked and updated systematically\\n4. This approach prevents missing any occurrences and maintains code consistency\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: I need to implement these features for my e-commerce site: user registration, product catalog, shopping cart, and checkout flow.\\nAssistant: I'll help implement these features. First, let's add all the features to the todo list.\\n*Creates a todo list breaking down each feature into specific tasks based on the project architecture*\\nAssistant: Let's start with user registration. This will involve creating database models, API endpoints, and frontend forms that integrate with your existing authentication system.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. The user provided multiple complex features to implement in a comma separated list\\n2. The todo list helps organize these large features into manageable tasks\\n3. This approach allows for tracking progress across the entire implementation\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Can you help optimize my React application? It's rendering slowly and has performance issues.</user>\\nAssistant: I'll help optimize your React application. First, let me examine your codebase to identify potential performance bottlenecks.\\n*Reviews component structure, render patterns, state management, and data fetching*\\nAssistant: After analyzing your codebase, I've identified several performance issues. Let me create a todo list to track our optimization efforts.\\n*Creates todo list with items like: 1) Implement memoization for expensive calculations in ProductList, 2) Add virtualization for long lists in Dashboard, 3) Optimize image loading in Gallery component, 4) Fix state update loops in ShoppingCart, 5) Review bundle size and implement code splitting*\\nLet's start by implementing memoization for the expensive calculations in your ProductList component.</assistant>\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. First, the assistant examined the codebase to identify specific performance issues\\n2. Based on this analysis, it identified multiple optimization opportunities\\n3. Performance optimization is a non-trivial task requiring multiple steps\\n4. The todo list helps methodically track improvements across different components\\n5. This systematic approach ensures all performance bottlenecks are addressed\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n## Examples of When NOT to Use the Todo List\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: How do I print 'Hello World' in Python?\\nAssistant: In Python, you can print \\\"Hello World\\\" with this simple code:\\n\\npython\\nprint(\\\"Hello World\\\")\\n\\nThis will output the text \\\"Hello World\\\" to the console when executed.</assistant>\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, trivial task that can be completed in one step. There's no need to track multiple tasks or steps for such a straightforward request.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: What does the git status command do?\\nAssistant: The git status command shows the current state of your working directory and staging area. It displays which changes have been staged, which haven't, and which files aren't being tracked by Git. This is a fundamental command for understanding the current state of your repository before making commits.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is an informational request with no actual coding task to complete. The user is simply asking for an explanation, not for the assistant to perform multiple steps or tasks.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Can you add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does?\\nAssistant: Sure, let me add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does.\\n* Uses the Edit tool to add a comment to the calculateTotal function *\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, straightforward task confined to one location in the code. Adding a comment doesn't require tracking multiple steps or systematic organization.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Run npm install for me and tell me what happens.\\nAssistant: I'll run the npm install command for you.\\n\\n*Executes: npm install*\\n\\nThe command completed successfully. Here's the output:\\n[Output of npm install command]\\n\\nAll dependencies have been installed according to your package.json file.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single command execution with immediate results. There are no multiple steps to track or organize, making the todo list unnecessary for this straightforward task.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n## Task States and Management\\n\\n1. **Task States**: Use these states to track progress:\\n   - pending: Task not yet started\\n   - in_progress: Currently working on (limit to ONE task at a time)\\n   - completed: Task finished successfully\\n   - cancelled: Task no longer needed\\n\\n2. **Task Management**:\\n   - Update task status in real-time as you work\\n   - Mark tasks complete IMMEDIATELY after finishing (don't batch completions)\\n   - Only have ONE task in_progress at any time\\n   - Complete current tasks before starting new ones\\n   - Cancel tasks that become irrelevant\\n\\n3. **Task Breakdown**:\\n   - Create specific, actionable items\\n   - Break complex tasks into smaller, manageable steps\\n   - Use clear, descriptive task names\\n\\nWhen in doubt, use this tool. Being proactive with task management demonstrates attentiveness and ensures you complete all requirements successfully.\\n\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"todos\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Brief description of the task\"},\"status\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Current status of the task: pending, in_progress, completed, cancelled\"},\"priority\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Priority level of the task: high, medium, low\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Unique identifier for the todo item\"}},\"required\":[\"content\",\"status\",\"priority\",\"id\"],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"description\":\"The updated todo list\"}},\"required\":[\"todos\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"todoread\",\"description\":\"Use this tool to read your todo list\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{},\"additionalProperties\":false}}}],\"tool_choice\":\"auto\",\"stream\":true,\"stream_options\":{\"include_usage\":true}}",
